  user_input {
    user_response: "# MASTER PROMPT \342\200\224 CLOAK: THE INVISIBLE GUARDIAN\n## Native Android implementation based on the original Cloak architecture\n\nAct as a senior Android engineer, Kotlin developer, mobile security engineer, and technical project lead. Build **Cloak \342\200\224 The Invisible Guardian**, an Android-only personal safety application.\n\nI am a beginner, so implementation must be staged, testable, documented, and easy to continue if an AI usage limit is reached.\n\n## 1. NON-NEGOTIABLE TECHNOLOGY REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Native Android application using **Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, and the Android SDK**.\n- Use Android Studio-compatible Gradle configuration and a proper native Android project.\n- Do NOT use Flutter, Dart, React Native, a WebView-based application, or a web app as the implementation.\n- Project location on my Windows computer: `D:\\CloakNative`.\n- Do not overwrite, modify, or delete my existing projects:\n  - `D:\\Cloak\\cloak_app`\n  - `D:\\CloakFresh\\cloak_app`\n  - `D:\\CloakFresh\\cloak_app_stage1_backup_20261024`\n- Use Supabase free-tier services for backend storage and functions where required. Do not introduce Firebase Storage, paid SMS APIs, paid maps APIs, or mandatory paid services.\n- Google Maps links must work without requiring a Google Maps API key.\n- Never place Supabase service-role keys, private credentials, or secrets in the Android application. Use only the appropriate public/anon key with restrictive Row Level Security (RLS) policies.\n- Keep the internal project name Cloak. The public-facing app may use a discreet name such as \342\200\234Petal\342\200\235.\n\n## 2. PRODUCT PURPOSE AND THREE-LAYER ARCHITECTURE\n\nCloak has three layers. Preserve all three; do not silently remove features or replace them with unrelated alternatives.\n\n### LAYER 1 \342\200\224 PUBLIC DISGUISE: PERIOD AND HEALTH TRACKER\n\nCreate a convincing, polished period-tracking interface that appears to be an ordinary health app.\n\nRequired features:\n\n1. Bottom navigation with Calendar, Log, Tips, and Settings.\n2. Calendar displaying:\n   - Marked period days in red.\n   - Predicted next period days in light pink.\n   - A dot on days with logged symptoms.\n3. Allow the user to mark period start and end dates.\n4. Cycle prediction based on the user\'s average cycle length, defaulting to 28 days.\n5. Symptom logging through selectable chips and a mood log.\n6. A scrolling Tips screen containing 15 static health-tip cards. If implementation time becomes constrained, five cards are an acceptable documented fallback.\n7. Settings for emergency contacts and relevant preferences.\n8. A discreet launcher label and icon, such as \342\200\234Petal\342\200\235, while keeping Cloak as the internal project name.\n9. A \342\200\234Call Gynecologist\342\200\235 card that launches a simulated incoming call screen.\n10. The simulated call should look convincing and use a configurable caller name. A normal tap can display \342\200\234Mom\342\200\235; a long press can display \342\200\234Police\342\200\235. Use an available ringtone where permitted, and provide a graceful fallback.\n\nThe tracker must remain accessible after entering either PIN. The duress PIN must not show a different screen, warning, dialog, or obvious indication that SOS has started.\n\n### LAYER 2 \342\200\224 GUARDIAN AND SOS\n\nThis is the highest-priority safety layer.\n\n#### A. Normal PIN and duress PIN\n\n- First-run setup must allow the user to configure a normal PIN and a separate duress PIN.\n- The architecture\'s demo values are normal PIN `1234` and duress PIN `9999`. Treat these as test/demo values only, not secure production defaults.\n- Both PINs must open the same tracker screen.\n- Entering the normal PIN must not start SOS.\n- Entering the duress PIN must silently initiate SOS in the background while displaying the same tracker.\n- Use a custom four-digit keypad.\n- Store PIN verifiers using a cryptographically secure, salted derivation method. Do not store plaintext PINs or use a fast unsalted hash. Use a modern password-based KDF supported by the Android/JVM implementation, with a random salt and constant-time verifier comparison.\n- Keep normal-PIN and duress-PIN verification logically distinct.\n- Handle first-run setup, PIN changes, app restarts, and verification errors safely.\n\n#### B. Shake-to-SOS\n\n- Use Android\'s gravity-free accelerometer readings.\n- Detect three qualifying acceleration spikes, each at least 250 milliseconds apart, within a 1.5-second window.\n- Begin with an acceleration threshold around 18 m/s\302\262, then expose it as a configurable/testable parameter.\n- Trigger the same central SOS controller used by the duress PIN.\n- Reduce accidental triggers by testing normal walking and phone movement.\n- Shake detection is expected to work only while the app is in the foreground for this MVP. Do not claim it works after the app is killed or while always in the background.\n\n#### C. Emergency contacts and SMS\n\n- Provide a Support Circle/emergency-contact screen.\n- Support up to three emergency contacts.\n- Use Android\'s native SMS functionality (`SmsManager`) when permitted and appropriate.\n- Request and explain required runtime permissions.\n- During SOS, send an emergency SMS containing a Google Maps URL using the best available location coordinates.\n- The map URL should follow this pattern: `https://maps.google.com/?q=LATITUDE,LONGITUDE`.\n- Use the last known location first when available, then obtain a fresh location if permitted.\n- Provide location updates approximately every two minutes while the SOS session is active and Android lifecycle/permission constraints allow.\n- Track the trigger source, such as `duress_pin` or `shake`.\n- The SOS controller must be centralized and idempotent: repeated triggers must not create duplicate uncontrolled sessions or duplicate sends.\n- The normal PIN stops an active SOS. The duress PIN returns to the tracker while SOS remains active.\n- Handle denied permissions, unavailable GPS, missing SIM, carrier failures, and SMS failures. Never claim delivery is guaranteed.\n- Clearly distinguish native SMS sending from opening an SMS composer. Do not substitute a user-mediated composer for the required native SMS path without documenting the change.\n- Google Play may restrict SMS permissions and distribution; document this limitation.\n\n#### D. Audio recording and Supabase upload\n\n- During SOS, capture audio in short chunks of approximately five seconds, uploading them to Supabase Storage.\n- Store SOS session metadata and location records in Supabase.\n- Implement retries for transient network failures and handle airplane mode without crashing or losing track of pending uploads.\n- Keep uploaded files associated with the correct SOS session.\n- Explain recording behaviour to the user and obtain required permissions and consent.\n- Respect Android microphone privacy indicators, foreground-service requirements, notification requirements, and Android version restrictions.\n- Android 14 and later impose background microphone restrictions. Do not claim that recording will continue invisibly under every background or screen-off condition.\n- If the OS does not permit recording in the requested state, stop or pause recording safely and show the appropriate system-required notification/permission state.\n- Never bypass Android privacy controls.\n\n#### E. Fake-Sleep / black screen\n\n- After approximately 15 seconds of inactivity during an active SOS, display a completely black screen that resembles a locked/inactive phone.\n- Keep SOS state separate from the visible screen state.\n- The exit gesture is a three-second hold followed by PIN entry.\n- Entering the normal PIN stops SOS and returns to the tracker.\n- Entering the duress PIN returns to the tracker without stopping SOS.\n- The black screen must not be represented as a guarantee that Android will continue background recording.\n- Document that Fake-Sleep is intended for the supported in-app lifecycle; do not promise it works after the process is killed.\n\n#### F. Optional WhatsApp relay\n\n- Provide an optional `wa.me` link for emergency-contact communication.\n- Opening WhatsApp requires the user to tap the link and complete any required interaction.\n- Do not claim WhatsApp messages can be sent silently without user action.\n\n### LAYER 3 \342\200\224 DIGITAL SHIELD\n\nThe Shield is entered through a hidden gesture, not a normal settings route.\n\n#### A. Secret gesture and navigation\n\n- The user must press and hold the small logo in the top-left of the tracker for three full seconds.\n- Cancel the gesture if the finger moves more than 20 pixels, or the user releases/cancels before the three seconds.\n- A short haptic vibration confirms entry.\n- The Shield opens as a single dashboard containing three tiles: Photo Sanitizer, Digital Lockdown, and Burner Notes.\n- There must be no tooltip, visible hint, or ordinary Settings entry for the Shield.\n- Disable entry while SOS is active.\n- Automatically close the Shield and return to the root screen when the app enters the background.\n\n#### B. Photo Sanitizer\n\n- Allow the user to select a photo from the device.\n- Remove EXIF metadata, especially GPS coordinates and device details.\n- Re-encode or otherwise produce a clean output file; do not simply rename the original.\n- Save a clean copy to an appropriate gallery/media location with user permission.\n- Verify the output metadata before reporting success.\n- If verification cannot be performed, clearly report that the result could not be verified.\n- The demo may show a count of removed tags and a clean copy saved to the gallery. If counting all tags is too time-consuming, prioritise producing and verifying a metadata-free copy.\n\n#### C. Digital Lockdown\n\n- Open Android system panels/settings for Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, and Location so the user can disable them.\n- Android does not generally permit ordinary apps to silently toggle these radios and settings. Do not attempt to bypass this restriction.\n- Clearly label this feature as a shortcut to system controls, not an automatic device-wide kill switch.\n\n#### D. Burner Notes\n\n- Allow a user to write a note and generate a random six-digit retrieval code.\n- Store the note in Supabase.\n- A note must be readable only once: retrieval and deletion must happen atomically on the server.\n- Notes must expire after 24 hours.\n- Reject invalid, expired, and already-used codes.\n- Add appropriate rate limiting and safe error handling.\n- Use a Supabase database function such as `read_and_burn(code)` to delete and return a note in a single atomic operation.\n- Enable RLS. The client must not have a general policy allowing it to read every note.\n- Do not expose a service-role key in the app.\n- After a successful retrieval, a second attempt must display a message such as \342\200\234This note has been destroyed.\342\200\235\n\n## 3. SUPABASE BACKEND REQUIREMENTS\n\nUse Supabase for SOS session records, location history, audio chunks, and burner notes.\n\nPlan the following logical data structures:\n\n- `sos_sessions`: session ID, trigger source, start timestamp, and required session status.\n- `sos_locations`: session ID, latitude, longitude, accuracy, and timestamp.\n- A private Storage bucket for SOS audio chunks, with access rules appropriate to the chosen authentication/security model.\n- `burner_notes`: six-digit code, note body, and creation timestamp.\n- A database function for atomic one-time note retrieval and deletion.\n\nSecurity requirements:\n\n- Enable RLS on tables containing user or safety data.\n- Define restrictive, documented policies for every table and Storage bucket.\n- Never allow unrestricted public reads of location history or audio.\n- Never expose service-role credentials in client code.\n- Validate input and rate-limit sensitive operations.\n- Explain the limitations of anonymous client access and propose a secure authentication/session design appropriate for the MVP.\n- Keep only the minimum data needed. Do not retain contractually or personally sensitive data unnecessarily.\n- Include SQL migrations/schema and instructions for configuring Supabase.\n- Keep secrets in local configuration/environment files ignored by Git. Provide a safe example configuration file without real credentials.\n\n## 4. TECHNICAL DESIGN AND CODE QUALITY\n\n- Use a clear separation between Compose UI, state/view models, domain logic, platform integrations, and backend services.\n- Create one central SOS controller/state machine. Duress PIN and shake-to-SOS must call the same implementation.\n- Make critical operations safe against duplicate taps, lifecycle changes, retries, and process restarts.\n- Keep UI state and safety-session state separate.\n- Use Kotlin coroutines and lifecycle-aware components appropriately.\n- Request permissions only when needed and explain why they are required.\n- Handle unsupported features and denied permissions without crashing.\n- Add unit tests for business logic and instrumentation/manual tests for Android platform features.\n- Do not generate a giant single-file implementation.\n- Avoid unnecessary libraries and paid dependencies.\n- Include loading, success, error, and permission-denied states.\n- Use clear names and beginner-friendly comments where they help.\n- Do not claim code compiles or tests pass unless you actually ran the relevant checks and observed the results.\n\n## 5. IMPLEMENTATION STAGES \342\200\224 DO NOT SKIP AHEAD\n\n### Stage 0 \342\200\224 Architecture mapping and feasibility\n\nBefore generating implementation code:\n\n1. Restate the architecture in your own words.\n2. Create a feature traceability table mapping every requirement in this prompt to a planned module, implementation stage, and test.\n3. Identify Android permission, lifecycle, security, backend, and distribution limitations.\n4. Propose the native Kotlin project structure and Gradle/dependency plan.\n5. List all required Android permissions and justify each one.\n6. Identify the Supabase schema, RLS policies, Storage configuration, and server functions.\n7. Give exact Windows PowerShell commands to create/open/build/test the separate project.\n8. Define what can be fully implemented, what needs a real-device test, and what is constrained by Android.\n9. Create a checklist of acceptance criteria for every stage.\n\nDo not implement the app yet. Wait for my approval of Stage 0.\n\n### Stage 1 \342\200\224 Project foundation and secure PIN\n\n- Create the native Android project and Compose shell.\n- Implement first-run setup, the normal PIN, the duress PIN, and the custom keypad.\n- Both PINs must lead to the same tracker shell.\n- The duress PIN must activate the SOS controller.\n- Add secure PIN derivation and verification.\n- Add tests for valid and invalid PINs, PIN separation, duress triggering, repeated submission, and persisted verification.\n- Build and run the app before marking Stage 1 complete.\n\n### Stage 2 \342\200\224 Core SOS, contacts, location, and SMS\n\n- Implement the Support Circle screen.\n- Implement the central SOS controller/state machine.\n- Add shake detection and duress-trigger integration.\n- Add location acquisition, Maps URL generation, native SMS, and approximately two-minute updates when permitted.\n- Test on a real Android phone with permission-denied, GPS-disabled, and SMS/network failure scenarios.\n- Do not proceed until the core SOS path is tested.\n\n### Stage 3 \342\200\224 Audio chunks, upload, and Fake-Sleep\n\n- Implement supported foreground audio recording and five-second chunking.\n- Configure Supabase Storage and session/location persistence with appropriate security.\n- Add upload retries and offline error handling.\n- Implement the 15-second inactivity black screen and three-second hold-plus-PIN exit.\n- Test supported lifecycle conditions on a real phone and document unsupported conditions.\n\n### Stage 4 \342\200\224 Tracker and simulated call\n\n- Complete the calendar, period marking, cycle prediction, symptom and mood logging, Tips, Settings, discreet branding, and emergency contacts.\n- Implement the \342\200\234Call Gynecologist\342\200\235 simulated incoming-call experience with configurable caller name.\n- Test the normal PIN and duress PIN both return to the same-looking tracker.\n\n### Stage 5 \342\200\224 Digital Shield\n\n- Implement the three-second logo hold with 20-pixel movement cancellation, haptic confirmation, SOS lockout, and automatic closure on backgrounding.\n- Implement photo sanitization and metadata verification.\n- Implement system-settings panels for Digital Lockdown.\n- Implement Burner Notes with random six-digit codes, atomic read-and-delete, 24-hour expiry, rate limiting, and RLS.\n- Test each feature independently.\n\n### Stage 6 \342\200\224 Integration, hardening, and demo\n\n- Run all automated tests and fix failures.\n- Perform an end-to-end test of the normal PIN, duress PIN, SMS, Maps link, supported audio upload, Fake-Sleep, shake-to-SOS, Photo Sanitizer, Digital Lockdown, and Burner Notes.\n- Verify behaviour on a real Android phone.\n- Prepare a release APK only after the build succeeds.\n- Provide a concise test report distinguishing verified, partially verified, and unverified behaviour.\n- Provide a 90-second demo script and explicitly disclose Android limitations.\n\n## 6. TIME AND PRIORITY RULES\n\nThe original architecture describes a 24-hour MVP and prioritises the life-critical SOS chain first.\n\nSuggested order:\n- Hours 0\342\200\2231: toolchain, native project, manifest, Supabase preparation.\n- Hours 1\342\200\2233: project shell and PIN setup/verification.\n- Hours 3\342\200\2235: emergency contacts and native SMS.\n- Hours 5\342\200\2237: location, Maps link, SOS state controller, updates.\n- Hours 7\342\200\22310: audio chunks, uploads, backend policies.\n- Hours 10\342\200\22312: Fake-Sleep, exit PIN, shake detection.\n- Hour 12\342\200\22313: real-device SOS checkpoint.\n- Hours 13\342\200\22318: tracker, simulated call, and hidden Shield entry.\n- Hours 18\342\200\22322: photo sanitizer, lockdown panels, and burner notes.\n- Hours 22\342\200\22324: integration, security checks, APK, demo rehearsal, and backup video.\n\nThese are targets, not a guarantee that all features can be completed in 24 hours. Prefer a tested, honest MVP over untested generated code.\n\nIf time runs short, acceptable documented simplifications include:\n- Fixed 28-day cycle prediction instead of average-based prediction.\n- Five tip cards instead of 15.\n- Skipping periodic SMS updates while retaining the first emergency SMS.\n- Skipping the before/after tag count while still saving and verifying a clean photo.\n- Simplifying expiry cleanup while retaining one-time note retrieval and 24-hour expiry enforcement.\n- One simulated caller screen with a configurable caller name.\n- WhatsApp relay for only the first contact.\n\nDo not silently remove the normal/duress PIN distinction, native emergency SMS path, supported audio chunk upload, Fake-Sleep screen, or shake-to-SOS. If Android restrictions prevent any behaviour, explain the limitation and provide the closest compliant implementation.\n\n## 7. REQUIRED OUTPUT AT THE END OF EVERY STAGE\n\nAfter each stage, report:\n\n1. **Stage status:** Not started / In progress / Complete and verified.\n2. Files created or changed.\n3. Exact PowerShell commands to build and test.\n4. Tests actually run and their actual results.\n5. Any remaining bugs, limitations, or unverified behaviour.\n6. How to save or back up the working code before continuing.\n7. The exact next action required from me.\n\nKeep each stage small. Do not regenerate files that already work unless necessary. Do not mark a stage complete merely because code was generated.\n\n## FIRST RESPONSE \342\200\224 STRICT INSTRUCTIONS\n\nBegin with Stage 0 only.\n\nReturn the feature traceability table, native project structure, Android feasibility/permission assessment, Supabase plan, stage acceptance criteria, and Windows setup/test strategy.\n\nDo not generate the full application, do not begin Stage 1, and do not assume that a feature works merely because it appears in a plan.\n\nWait for my explicit approval before implementation."
    items {
      item {
        text_block {
          content: "---\ntype: \"feature\"\ntitle: \"Build an Android app\"\nicon: \"android_color\"\n---\n\n"
        }
      }
    }
    items {
      text: "# MASTER PROMPT \342\200\224 CLOAK: THE INVISIBLE GUARDIAN\n## Native Android implementation based on the original Cloak architecture\n\nAct as a senior Android engineer, Kotlin developer, mobile security engineer, and technical project lead. Build **Cloak \342\200\224 The Invisible Guardian**, an Android-only personal safety application.\n\nI am a beginner, so implementation must be staged, testable, documented, and easy to continue if an AI usage limit is reached.\n\n## 1. NON-NEGOTIABLE TECHNOLOGY REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Native Android application using **Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, and the Android SDK**.\n- Use Android Studio-compatible Gradle configuration and a proper native Android project.\n- Do NOT use Flutter, Dart, React Native, a WebView-based application, or a web app as the implementation.\n- Project location on my Windows computer: `D:\\CloakNative`.\n- Do not overwrite, modify, or delete my existing projects:\n  - `D:\\Cloak\\cloak_app`\n  - `D:\\CloakFresh\\cloak_app`\n  - `D:\\CloakFresh\\cloak_app_stage1_backup_20261024`\n- Use Supabase free-tier services for backend storage and functions where required. Do not introduce Firebase Storage, paid SMS APIs, paid maps APIs, or mandatory paid services.\n- Google Maps links must work without requiring a Google Maps API key.\n- Never place Supabase service-role keys, private credentials, or secrets in the Android application. Use only the appropriate public/anon key with restrictive Row Level Security (RLS) policies.\n- Keep the internal project name Cloak. The public-facing app may use a discreet name such as \342\200\234Petal\342\200\235.\n\n## 2. PRODUCT PURPOSE AND THREE-LAYER ARCHITECTURE\n\nCloak has three layers. Preserve all three; do not silently remove features or replace them with unrelated alternatives.\n\n### LAYER 1 \342\200\224 PUBLIC DISGUISE: PERIOD AND HEALTH TRACKER\n\nCreate a convincing, polished period-tracking interface that appears to be an ordinary health app.\n\nRequired features:\n\n1. Bottom navigation with Calendar, Log, Tips, and Settings.\n2. Calendar displaying:\n   - Marked period days in red.\n   - Predicted next period days in light pink.\n   - A dot on days with logged symptoms.\n3. Allow the user to mark period start and end dates.\n4. Cycle prediction based on the user\'s average cycle length, defaulting to 28 days.\n5. Symptom logging through selectable chips and a mood log.\n6. A scrolling Tips screen containing 15 static health-tip cards. If implementation time becomes constrained, five cards are an acceptable documented fallback.\n7. Settings for emergency contacts and relevant preferences.\n8. A discreet launcher label and icon, such as \342\200\234Petal\342\200\235, while keeping Cloak as the internal project name.\n9. A \342\200\234Call Gynecologist\342\200\235 card that launches a simulated incoming call screen.\n10. The simulated call should look convincing and use a configurable caller name. A normal tap can display \342\200\234Mom\342\200\235; a long press can display \342\200\234Police\342\200\235. Use an available ringtone where permitted, and provide a graceful fallback.\n\nThe tracker must remain accessible after entering either PIN. The duress PIN must not show a different screen, warning, dialog, or obvious indication that SOS has started.\n\n### LAYER 2 \342\200\224 GUARDIAN AND SOS\n\nThis is the highest-priority safety layer.\n\n#### A. Normal PIN and duress PIN\n\n- First-run setup must allow the user to configure a normal PIN and a separate duress PIN.\n- The architecture\'s demo values are normal PIN `1234` and duress PIN `9999`. Treat these as test/demo values only, not secure production defaults.\n- Both PINs must open the same tracker screen.\n- Entering the normal PIN must not start SOS.\n- Entering the duress PIN must silently initiate SOS in the background while displaying the same tracker.\n- Use a custom four-digit keypad.\n- Store PIN verifiers using a cryptographically secure, salted derivation method. Do not store plaintext PINs or use a fast unsalted hash. Use a modern password-based KDF supported by the Android/JVM implementation, with a random salt and constant-time verifier comparison.\n- Keep normal-PIN and duress-PIN verification logically distinct.\n- Handle first-run setup, PIN changes, app restarts, and verification errors safely.\n\n#### B. Shake-to-SOS\n\n- Use Android\'s gravity-free accelerometer readings.\n- Detect three qualifying acceleration spikes, each at least 250 milliseconds apart, within a 1.5-second window.\n- Begin with an acceleration threshold around 18 m/s\302\262, then expose it as a configurable/testable parameter.\n- Trigger the same central SOS controller used by the duress PIN.\n- Reduce accidental triggers by testing normal walking and phone movement.\n- Shake detection is expected to work only while the app is in the foreground for this MVP. Do not claim it works after the app is killed or while always in the background.\n\n#### C. Emergency contacts and SMS\n\n- Provide a Support Circle/emergency-contact screen.\n- Support up to three emergency contacts.\n- Use Android\'s native SMS functionality (`SmsManager`) when permitted and appropriate.\n- Request and explain required runtime permissions.\n- During SOS, send an emergency SMS containing a Google Maps URL using the best available location coordinates.\n- The map URL should follow this pattern: `https://maps.google.com/?q=LATITUDE,LONGITUDE`.\n- Use the last known location first when available, then obtain a fresh location if permitted.\n- Provide location updates approximately every two minutes while the SOS session is active and Android lifecycle/permission constraints allow.\n- Track the trigger source, such as `duress_pin` or `shake`.\n- The SOS controller must be centralized and idempotent: repeated triggers must not create duplicate uncontrolled sessions or duplicate sends.\n- The normal PIN stops an active SOS. The duress PIN returns to the tracker while SOS remains active.\n- Handle denied permissions, unavailable GPS, missing SIM, carrier failures, and SMS failures. Never claim delivery is guaranteed.\n- Clearly distinguish native SMS sending from opening an SMS composer. Do not substitute a user-mediated composer for the required native SMS path without documenting the change.\n- Google Play may restrict SMS permissions and distribution; document this limitation.\n\n#### D. Audio recording and Supabase upload\n\n- During SOS, capture audio in short chunks of approximately five seconds, uploading them to Supabase Storage.\n- Store SOS session metadata and location records in Supabase.\n- Implement retries for transient network failures and handle airplane mode without crashing or losing track of pending uploads.\n- Keep uploaded files associated with the correct SOS session.\n- Explain recording behaviour to the user and obtain required permissions and consent.\n- Respect Android microphone privacy indicators, foreground-service requirements, notification requirements, and Android version restrictions.\n- Android 14 and later impose background microphone restrictions. Do not claim that recording will continue invisibly under every background or screen-off condition.\n- If the OS does not permit recording in the requested state, stop or pause recording safely and show the appropriate system-required notification/permission state.\n- Never bypass Android privacy controls.\n\n#### E. Fake-Sleep / black screen\n\n- After approximately 15 seconds of inactivity during an active SOS, display a completely black screen that resembles a locked/inactive phone.\n- Keep SOS state separate from the visible screen state.\n- The exit gesture is a three-second hold followed by PIN entry.\n- Entering the normal PIN stops SOS and returns to the tracker.\n- Entering the duress PIN returns to the tracker without stopping SOS.\n- The black screen must not be represented as a guarantee that Android will continue background recording.\n- Document that Fake-Sleep is intended for the supported in-app lifecycle; do not promise it works after the process is killed.\n\n#### F. Optional WhatsApp relay\n\n- Provide an optional `wa.me` link for emergency-contact communication.\n- Opening WhatsApp requires the user to tap the link and complete any required interaction.\n- Do not claim WhatsApp messages can be sent silently without user action.\n\n### LAYER 3 \342\200\224 DIGITAL SHIELD\n\nThe Shield is entered through a hidden gesture, not a normal settings route.\n\n#### A. Secret gesture and navigation\n\n- The user must press and hold the small logo in the top-left of the tracker for three full seconds.\n- Cancel the gesture if the finger moves more than 20 pixels, or the user releases/cancels before the three seconds.\n- A short haptic vibration confirms entry.\n- The Shield opens as a single dashboard containing three tiles: Photo Sanitizer, Digital Lockdown, and Burner Notes.\n- There must be no tooltip, visible hint, or ordinary Settings entry for the Shield.\n- Disable entry while SOS is active.\n- Automatically close the Shield and return to the root screen when the app enters the background.\n\n#### B. Photo Sanitizer\n\n- Allow the user to select a photo from the device.\n- Remove EXIF metadata, especially GPS coordinates and device details.\n- Re-encode or otherwise produce a clean output file; do not simply rename the original.\n- Save a clean copy to an appropriate gallery/media location with user permission.\n- Verify the output metadata before reporting success.\n- If verification cannot be performed, clearly report that the result could not be verified.\n- The demo may show a count of removed tags and a clean copy saved to the gallery. If counting all tags is too time-consuming, prioritise producing and verifying a metadata-free copy.\n\n#### C. Digital Lockdown\n\n- Open Android system panels/settings for Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, and Location so the user can disable them.\n- Android does not generally permit ordinary apps to silently toggle these radios and settings. Do not attempt to bypass this restriction.\n- Clearly label this feature as a shortcut to system controls, not an automatic device-wide kill switch.\n\n#### D. Burner Notes\n\n- Allow a user to write a note and generate a random six-digit retrieval code.\n- Store the note in Supabase.\n- A note must be readable only once: retrieval and deletion must happen atomically on the server.\n- Notes must expire after 24 hours.\n- Reject invalid, expired, and already-used codes.\n- Add appropriate rate limiting and safe error handling.\n- Use a Supabase database function such as `read_and_burn(code)` to delete and return a note in a single atomic operation.\n- Enable RLS. The client must not have a general policy allowing it to read every note.\n- Do not expose a service-role key in the app.\n- After a successful retrieval, a second attempt must display a message such as \342\200\234This note has been destroyed.\342\200\235\n\n## 3. SUPABASE BACKEND REQUIREMENTS\n\nUse Supabase for SOS session records, location history, audio chunks, and burner notes.\n\nPlan the following logical data structures:\n\n- `sos_sessions`: session ID, trigger source, start timestamp, and required session status.\n- `sos_locations`: session ID, latitude, longitude, accuracy, and timestamp.\n- A private Storage bucket for SOS audio chunks, with access rules appropriate to the chosen authentication/security model.\n- `burner_notes`: six-digit code, note body, and creation timestamp.\n- A database function for atomic one-time note retrieval and deletion.\n\nSecurity requirements:\n\n- Enable RLS on tables containing user or safety data.\n- Define restrictive, documented policies for every table and Storage bucket.\n- Never allow unrestricted public reads of location history or audio.\n- Never expose service-role credentials in client code.\n- Validate input and rate-limit sensitive operations.\n- Explain the limitations of anonymous client access and propose a secure authentication/session design appropriate for the MVP.\n- Keep only the minimum data needed. Do not retain contractually or personally sensitive data unnecessarily.\n- Include SQL migrations/schema and instructions for configuring Supabase.\n- Keep secrets in local configuration/environment files ignored by Git. Provide a safe example configuration file without real credentials.\n\n## 4. TECHNICAL DESIGN AND CODE QUALITY\n\n- Use a clear separation between Compose UI, state/view models, domain logic, platform integrations, and backend services.\n- Create one central SOS controller/state machine. Duress PIN and shake-to-SOS must call the same implementation.\n- Make critical operations safe against duplicate taps, lifecycle changes, retries, and process restarts.\n- Keep UI state and safety-session state separate.\n- Use Kotlin coroutines and lifecycle-aware components appropriately.\n- Request permissions only when needed and explain why they are required.\n- Handle unsupported features and denied permissions without crashing.\n- Add unit tests for business logic and instrumentation/manual tests for Android platform features.\n- Do not generate a giant single-file implementation.\n- Avoid unnecessary libraries and paid dependencies.\n- Include loading, success, error, and permission-denied states.\n- Use clear names and beginner-friendly comments where they help.\n- Do not claim code compiles or tests pass unless you actually ran the relevant checks and observed the results.\n\n## 5. IMPLEMENTATION STAGES \342\200\224 DO NOT SKIP AHEAD\n\n### Stage 0 \342\200\224 Architecture mapping and feasibility\n\nBefore generating implementation code:\n\n1. Restate the architecture in your own words.\n2. Create a feature traceability table mapping every requirement in this prompt to a planned module, implementation stage, and test.\n3. Identify Android permission, lifecycle, security, backend, and distribution limitations.\n4. Propose the native Kotlin project structure and Gradle/dependency plan.\n5. List all required Android permissions and justify each one.\n6. Identify the Supabase schema, RLS policies, Storage configuration, and server functions.\n7. Give exact Windows PowerShell commands to create/open/build/test the separate project.\n8. Define what can be fully implemented, what needs a real-device test, and what is constrained by Android.\n9. Create a checklist of acceptance criteria for every stage.\n\nDo not implement the app yet. Wait for my approval of Stage 0.\n\n### Stage 1 \342\200\224 Project foundation and secure PIN\n\n- Create the native Android project and Compose shell.\n- Implement first-run setup, the normal PIN, the duress PIN, and the custom keypad.\n- Both PINs must lead to the same tracker shell.\n- The duress PIN must activate the SOS controller.\n- Add secure PIN derivation and verification.\n- Add tests for valid and invalid PINs, PIN separation, duress triggering, repeated submission, and persisted verification.\n- Build and run the app before marking Stage 1 complete.\n\n### Stage 2 \342\200\224 Core SOS, contacts, location, and SMS\n\n- Implement the Support Circle screen.\n- Implement the central SOS controller/state machine.\n- Add shake detection and duress-trigger integration.\n- Add location acquisition, Maps URL generation, native SMS, and approximately two-minute updates when permitted.\n- Test on a real Android phone with permission-denied, GPS-disabled, and SMS/network failure scenarios.\n- Do not proceed until the core SOS path is tested.\n\n### Stage 3 \342\200\224 Audio chunks, upload, and Fake-Sleep\n\n- Implement supported foreground audio recording and five-second chunking.\n- Configure Supabase Storage and session/location persistence with appropriate security.\n- Add upload retries and offline error handling.\n- Implement the 15-second inactivity black screen and three-second hold-plus-PIN exit.\n- Test supported lifecycle conditions on a real phone and document unsupported conditions.\n\n### Stage 4 \342\200\224 Tracker and simulated call\n\n- Complete the calendar, period marking, cycle prediction, symptom and mood logging, Tips, Settings, discreet branding, and emergency contacts.\n- Implement the \342\200\234Call Gynecologist\342\200\235 simulated incoming-call experience with configurable caller name.\n- Test the normal PIN and duress PIN both return to the same-looking tracker.\n\n### Stage 5 \342\200\224 Digital Shield\n\n- Implement the three-second logo hold with 20-pixel movement cancellation, haptic confirmation, SOS lockout, and automatic closure on backgrounding.\n- Implement photo sanitization and metadata verification.\n- Implement system-settings panels for Digital Lockdown.\n- Implement Burner Notes with random six-digit codes, atomic read-and-delete, 24-hour expiry, rate limiting, and RLS.\n- Test each feature independently.\n\n### Stage 6 \342\200\224 Integration, hardening, and demo\n\n- Run all automated tests and fix failures.\n- Perform an end-to-end test of the normal PIN, duress PIN, SMS, Maps link, supported audio upload, Fake-Sleep, shake-to-SOS, Photo Sanitizer, Digital Lockdown, and Burner Notes.\n- Verify behaviour on a real Android phone.\n- Prepare a release APK only after the build succeeds.\n- Provide a concise test report distinguishing verified, partially verified, and unverified behaviour.\n- Provide a 90-second demo script and explicitly disclose Android limitations.\n\n## 6. TIME AND PRIORITY RULES\n\nThe original architecture describes a 24-hour MVP and prioritises the life-critical SOS chain first.\n\nSuggested order:\n- Hours 0\342\200\2231: toolchain, native project, manifest, Supabase preparation.\n- Hours 1\342\200\2233: project shell and PIN setup/verification.\n- Hours 3\342\200\2235: emergency contacts and native SMS.\n- Hours 5\342\200\2237: location, Maps link, SOS state controller, updates.\n- Hours 7\342\200\22310: audio chunks, uploads, backend policies.\n- Hours 10\342\200\22312: Fake-Sleep, exit PIN, shake detection.\n- Hour 12\342\200\22313: real-device SOS checkpoint.\n- Hours 13\342\200\22318: tracker, simulated call, and hidden Shield entry.\n- Hours 18\342\200\22322: photo sanitizer, lockdown panels, and burner notes.\n- Hours 22\342\200\22324: integration, security checks, APK, demo rehearsal, and backup video.\n\nThese are targets, not a guarantee that all features can be completed in 24 hours. Prefer a tested, honest MVP over untested generated code.\n\nIf time runs short, acceptable documented simplifications include:\n- Fixed 28-day cycle prediction instead of average-based prediction.\n- Five tip cards instead of 15.\n- Skipping periodic SMS updates while retaining the first emergency SMS.\n- Skipping the before/after tag count while still saving and verifying a clean photo.\n- Simplifying expiry cleanup while retaining one-time note retrieval and 24-hour expiry enforcement.\n- One simulated caller screen with a configurable caller name.\n- WhatsApp relay for only the first contact.\n\nDo not silently remove the normal/duress PIN distinction, native emergency SMS path, supported audio chunk upload, Fake-Sleep screen, or shake-to-SOS. If Android restrictions prevent any behaviour, explain the limitation and provide the closest compliant implementation.\n\n## 7. REQUIRED OUTPUT AT THE END OF EVERY STAGE\n\nAfter each stage, report:\n\n1. **Stage status:** Not started / In progress / Complete and verified.\n2. Files created or changed.\n3. Exact PowerShell commands to build and test.\n4. Tests actually run and their actual results.\n5. Any remaining bugs, limitations, or unverified behaviour.\n6. How to save or back up the working code before continuing.\n7. The exact next action required from me.\n\nKeep each stage small. Do not regenerate files that already work unless necessary. Do not mark a stage complete merely because code was generated.\n\n## FIRST RESPONSE \342\200\224 STRICT INSTRUCTIONS\n\nBegin with Stage 0 only.\n\nReturn the feature traceability table, native project structure, Android feasibility/permission assessment, Supabase plan, stage acceptance criteria, and Windows setup/test strategy.\n\nDo not generate the full application, do not begin Stage 1, and do not assume that a feature works merely because it appears in a plan.\n\nWait for my explicit approval before implementation."
    }
  }
}
