    user_response: "# CLOAK \342\200\224 STAGE 2: PERIOD TRACKER DISGUISE + LIVE SOS FEATURES\n\nContinue working on my existing native Android application, **Cloak \342\200\224 The Invisible Guardian**, using Kotlin and Jetpack Compose.\n\n**Stage 0 is complete.** Preserve all verified work and existing architecture. Do not restart the project, replace working files unnecessarily, or repeat Stage 0.\n\nImplement **Stage 2 completely**, integrating with the Stage 1 code already present. First inspect the current project and identify the actual classes, permissions, services, and features that already exist. Reuse working implementations rather than creating duplicate systems.\n\n## 1. Primary requirement: Period-tracker disguise\n\nThe app must appear to be a normal period-tracking application from the Android home screen.\n\n- Use **Petal** as the public-facing app name.\n- Configure the Android launcher label and app icon to look like a period tracker, not a safety or SOS application.\n- Use a tasteful, simple menstrual-health icon and matching app theme.\n- When the app opens normally, immediately show the period-tracker interface.\n- Include bottom navigation: Calendar, Log, Tips, and Settings.\n- Calendar: mark period days and predict the next period using an average cycle length, defaulting to 28 days.\n- Log: record symptoms and mood.\n- Tips: show at least five basic period-health tips.\n- Settings: manage emergency contacts, cycle settings, and privacy options.\n- Keep emergency functionality accessible through the configured PIN and SOS workflows, not through an obvious SOS button on the public home screen.\n\nDo not leave the launcher name, launcher icon, splash screen, notifications, or app title visibly branded as \342\200\234Cloak\342\200\235 or \342\200\234SOS\342\200\235 in normal use. Do not impersonate another installed application.\n\n## 2. Emergency contacts: maximum three\n\nImplement contact management for up to three emergency contacts.\n\n- Allow adding, editing, deleting, and selecting contacts.\n- Store only the information needed for emergency delivery.\n- Validate phone numbers and handle missing or invalid contacts.\n- Request Android permissions at the appropriate time and explain why they are needed.\n- Ensure that the SOS controller uses the saved contacts consistently.\n- Handle unavailable SIM cards, missing network connectivity, permission denial, and SMS failures gracefully.\n\n## 3. Live location sharing\n\nImplement the real SOS location workflow for all configured emergency contacts, up to three.\n\nWhen SOS starts:\n\n1. Obtain the best available location using Android\'s supported location APIs.\n2. If a last-known location is available, send it promptly while attempting to obtain a fresh location.\n3. Send an SMS to each configured contact containing a Google Maps link in this format:\n\n   `https://maps.google.com/?q=LATITUDE,LONGITUDE`\n\n4. Include a timestamp and make clear whether the location is recent or last known.\n5. While the SOS session remains active, refresh the location approximately every two minutes when Android permits.\n6. Share updates with all configured contacts, with sensible rate limiting and retry handling.\n7. Stop location updates when the SOS controller confirms that the session has ended.\n\nUse native Android location APIs and the existing SOS controller. Do not use a paid Maps API or paid SMS provider.\n\nDo not claim that SMS or live tracking is guaranteed: delivery depends on permissions, SIM, carrier, network, and Android background-execution limits. Handle these limitations visibly in diagnostics.\n\n## 4. SOS audio recording in approximately five-second chunks\n\nIntegrate audio recording with the active SOS session.\n\n- Start recording only after the user has explicitly granted the required microphone permission and the SOS recording workflow has been activated.\n- Use a supported Android foreground-service approach where required, with the appropriate service type and notification.\n- Capture audio in approximately five-second chunks.\n- Upload each completed chunk securely to the existing Supabase backend.\n- Associate each chunk with the SOS session, a sequence number, and a timestamp.\n- Upload chunks as they are completed rather than waiting for the entire recording to finish.\n- Handle temporary network loss with a bounded retry queue and avoid duplicate uploads.\n- Protect audio using private storage and appropriate access controls.\n- Make the audio available to the authorized emergency workflow for the configured contacts. Do not expose public, unauthenticated audio URLs or send large audio files directly through SMS.\n- Provide an appropriate emergency notification or secure access mechanism for authorized contacts if the existing backend supports it.\n- Stop recording and release the microphone when SOS ends, subject to the defined safety workflow.\n\n**Important:** Android may restrict microphone access when an app is in the background. Implement only supported behavior and report failures honestly. Do not bypass Android permission prompts, microphone privacy indicators, or operating-system restrictions.\n\n## 5. Black-screen / Fake-Sleep interface during SOS\n\nImplement the configured Fake-Sleep interface for an active SOS session.\n\n- After approximately 15 seconds of inactivity during SOS, transition to a completely black app interface.\n- The black interface must not accidentally end the SOS session, stop supported recording, or stop location sharing.\n- Keep the active SOS controller independent of the visible screen.\n- Provide a controlled recovery path requiring a three-second press and PIN verification.\n- A normal PIN should stop SOS according to the existing architecture.\n- A duress PIN should return to the normal period-tracker interface while leaving the SOS session active.\n- Do not expose emergency details on the lock screen or in ordinary notifications.\n- Do not claim that a black app screen turns off the display, guarantees recording, or hides Android\'s system microphone indicators.\n\n## 6. Preserve Stage 1 security\n\nDo not weaken or replace the existing PIN security, duress PIN, contact storage, or idempotent SOS controller.\n\n- The normal PIN and duress PIN must continue to open the same period-tracker screen.\n- Entering the duress PIN must silently initiate the configured SOS workflow, without showing an obvious SOS screen.\n- Reuse the existing secure PIN hashing implementation.\n- Prevent repeated SOS initialization from duplicate events.\n- Ensure the black-screen interface and normal tracker UI do not accidentally reset an active SOS session.\n\n## 7. Supabase and privacy\n\nUse the existing Supabase configuration and schema wherever possible.\n\n- Store SOS sessions, location updates, and audio chunk metadata securely.\n- Use private storage buckets and restrictive Row Level Security policies.\n- Authorize contact access through the intended secure workflow.\n- Never put the Supabase service-role key in the Android app.\n- Never make sensitive audio or location records publicly readable.\n- Do not silently upload audio outside the explicitly activated SOS workflow.\n- Do not add Firebase Storage, paid services, or unnecessary dependencies.\n\n## 8. Stage 2 implementation requirements\n\nBefore coding, inspect the current project and produce a short inventory of:\n- Existing working Stage 1 components.\n- Existing Android permissions and manifest entries.\n- Existing Supabase configuration and backend functions.\n- Features that are missing or only placeholders.\n\nThen implement the missing Stage 2 functionality in the existing project.\n\nUpdate the launcher label and icon resources, Compose screens, Android manifest, location and recording services, SOS integration, Supabase integration, and tests as required.\n\nDo not create a fake implementation that merely changes the UI. The location-sharing, audio-chunk upload, contact handling, and SOS lifecycle must be wired to actual code.\n\n## 9. Verification checklist\n\nTest and report each item individually:\n\n1. The installed app displays **Petal** with a period-tracker icon on the home screen.\n2. Opening the app displays the tracker, not an SOS dashboard.\n3. Calendar, Log, Tips, and Settings work.\n4. Up to three emergency contacts can be configured.\n5. Starting SOS sends a location SMS to every configured contact when permissions and delivery conditions allow.\n6. Fresh location updates continue at the configured interval while supported.\n7. Audio is captured in approximately five-second chunks during an active, permitted SOS session.\n8. Each completed chunk is uploaded to private Supabase storage.\n9. Upload failures are handled without falsely reporting success.\n10. The black-screen interface activates after the configured inactivity period without ending SOS.\n11. PIN recovery follows the existing normal-PIN and duress-PIN behavior.\n12. Ending SOS stops supported recording and location updates.\n13. Tests verify important success, failure, permission-denial, and offline scenarios.\n14. The app builds successfully and can be installed on a real Android device.\n\nFor every item, report **PASS, FAIL, or NOT TESTED**. Do not mark a feature as working merely because the code compiles or the UI looks correct.\n\nAt the end, provide:\n- Files created or modified.\n- Exact commands to build and test the app.\n- Actual test results.\n- Android permissions or device setup required for testing.\n- Any Supabase schema, storage bucket, or function changes still needed.\n- Known limitations and incomplete functionality.\n\n**Work on Stage 2 now. Do not start Stage 3 until Stage 2 is verified.**"
    items {
      text: "# CLOAK \342\200\224 STAGE 2: PERIOD TRACKER DISGUISE + LIVE SOS FEATURES\n\nContinue working on my existing native Android application, **Cloak \342\200\224 The Invisible Guardian**, using Kotlin and Jetpack Compose.\n\n**Stage 0 is complete.** Preserve all verified work and existing architecture. Do not restart the project, replace working files unnecessarily, or repeat Stage 0.\n\nImplement **Stage 2 completely**, integrating with the Stage 1 code already present. First inspect the current project and identify the actual classes, permissions, services, and features that already exist. Reuse working implementations rather than creating duplicate systems.\n\n## 1. Primary requirement: Period-tracker disguise\n\nThe app must appear to be a normal period-tracking application from the Android home screen.\n\n- Use **Petal** as the public-facing app name.\n- Configure the Android launcher label and app icon to look like a period tracker, not a safety or SOS application.\n- Use a tasteful, simple menstrual-health icon and matching app theme.\n- When the app opens normally, immediately show the period-tracker interface.\n- Include bottom navigation: Calendar, Log, Tips, and Settings.\n- Calendar: mark period days and predict the next period using an average cycle length, defaulting to 28 days.\n- Log: record symptoms and mood.\n- Tips: show at least five basic period-health tips.\n- Settings: manage emergency contacts, cycle settings, and privacy options.\n- Keep emergency functionality accessible through the configured PIN and SOS workflows, not through an obvious SOS button on the public home screen.\n\nDo not leave the launcher name, launcher icon, splash screen, notifications, or app title visibly branded as \342\200\234Cloak\342\200\235 or \342\200\234SOS\342\200\235 in normal use. Do not impersonate another installed application.\n\n## 2. Emergency contacts: maximum three\n\nImplement contact management for up to three emergency contacts.\n\n- Allow adding, editing, deleting, and selecting contacts.\n- Store only the information needed for emergency delivery.\n- Validate phone numbers and handle missing or invalid contacts.\n- Request Android permissions at the appropriate time and explain why they are needed.\n- Ensure that the SOS controller uses the saved contacts consistently.\n- Handle unavailable SIM cards, missing network connectivity, permission denial, and SMS failures gracefully.\n\n## 3. Live location sharing\n\nImplement the real SOS location workflow for all configured emergency contacts, up to three.\n\nWhen SOS starts:\n\n1. Obtain the best available location using Android\'s supported location APIs.\n2. If a last-known location is available, send it promptly while attempting to obtain a fresh location.\n3. Send an SMS to each configured contact containing a Google Maps link in this format:\n\n   `https://maps.google.com/?q=LATITUDE,LONGITUDE`\n\n4. Include a timestamp and make clear whether the location is recent or last known.\n5. While the SOS session remains active, refresh the location approximately every two minutes when Android permits.\n6. Share updates with all configured contacts, with sensible rate limiting and retry handling.\n7. Stop location updates when the SOS controller confirms that the session has ended.\n\nUse native Android location APIs and the existing SOS controller. Do not use a paid Maps API or paid SMS provider.\n\nDo not claim that SMS or live tracking is guaranteed: delivery depends on permissions, SIM, carrier, network, and Android background-execution limits. Handle these limitations visibly in diagnostics.\n\n## 4. SOS audio recording in approximately five-second chunks\n\nIntegrate audio recording with the active SOS session.\n\n- Start recording only after the user has explicitly granted the required microphone permission and the SOS recording workflow has been activated.\n- Use a supported Android foreground-service approach where required, with the appropriate service type and notification.\n- Capture audio in approximately five-second chunks.\n- Upload each completed chunk securely to the existing Supabase backend.\n- Associate each chunk with the SOS session, a sequence number, and a timestamp.\n- Upload chunks as they are completed rather than waiting for the entire recording to finish.\n- Handle temporary network loss with a bounded retry queue and avoid duplicate uploads.\n- Protect audio using private storage and appropriate access controls.\n- Make the audio available to the authorized emergency workflow for the configured contacts. Do not expose public, unauthenticated audio URLs or send large audio files directly through SMS.\n- Provide an appropriate emergency notification or secure access mechanism for authorized contacts if the existing backend supports it.\n- Stop recording and release the microphone when SOS ends, subject to the defined safety workflow.\n\n**Important:** Android may restrict microphone access when an app is in the background. Implement only supported behavior and report failures honestly. Do not bypass Android permission prompts, microphone privacy indicators, or operating-system restrictions.\n\n## 5. Black-screen / Fake-Sleep interface during SOS\n\nImplement the configured Fake-Sleep interface for an active SOS session.\n\n- After approximately 15 seconds of inactivity during SOS, transition to a completely black app interface.\n- The black interface must not accidentally end the SOS session, stop supported recording, or stop location sharing.\n- Keep the active SOS controller independent of the visible screen.\n- Provide a controlled recovery path requiring a three-second press and PIN verification.\n- A normal PIN should stop SOS according to the existing architecture.\n- A duress PIN should return to the normal period-tracker interface while leaving the SOS session active.\n- Do not expose emergency details on the lock screen or in ordinary notifications.\n- Do not claim that a black app screen turns off the display, guarantees recording, or hides Android\'s system microphone indicators.\n\n## 6. Preserve Stage 1 security\n\nDo not weaken or replace the existing PIN security, duress PIN, contact storage, or idempotent SOS controller.\n\n- The normal PIN and duress PIN must continue to open the same period-tracker screen.\n- Entering the duress PIN must silently initiate the configured SOS workflow, without showing an obvious SOS screen.\n- Reuse the existing secure PIN hashing implementation.\n- Prevent repeated SOS initialization from duplicate events.\n- Ensure the black-screen interface and normal tracker UI do not accidentally reset an active SOS session.\n\n## 7. Supabase and privacy\n\nUse the existing Supabase configuration and schema wherever possible.\n\n- Store SOS sessions, location updates, and audio chunk metadata securely.\n- Use private storage buckets and restrictive Row Level Security policies.\n- Authorize contact access through the intended secure workflow.\n- Never put the Supabase service-role key in the Android app.\n- Never make sensitive audio or location records publicly readable.\n- Do not silently upload audio outside the explicitly activated SOS workflow.\n- Do not add Firebase Storage, paid services, or unnecessary dependencies.\n\n## 8. Stage 2 implementation requirements\n\nBefore coding, inspect the current project and produce a short inventory of:\n- Existing working Stage 1 components.\n- Existing Android permissions and manifest entries.\n- Existing Supabase configuration and backend functions.\n- Features that are missing or only placeholders.\n\nThen implement the missing Stage 2 functionality in the existing project.\n\nUpdate the launcher label and icon resources, Compose screens, Android manifest, location and recording services, SOS integration, Supabase integration, and tests as required.\n\nDo not create a fake implementation that merely changes the UI. The location-sharing, audio-chunk upload, contact handling, and SOS lifecycle must be wired to actual code.\n\n## 9. Verification checklist\n\nTest and report each item individually:\n\n1. The installed app displays **Petal** with a period-tracker icon on the home screen.\n2. Opening the app displays the tracker, not an SOS dashboard.\n3. Calendar, Log, Tips, and Settings work.\n4. Up to three emergency contacts can be configured.\n5. Starting SOS sends a location SMS to every configured contact when permissions and delivery conditions allow.\n6. Fresh location updates continue at the configured interval while supported.\n7. Audio is captured in approximately five-second chunks during an active, permitted SOS session.\n8. Each completed chunk is uploaded to private Supabase storage.\n9. Upload failures are handled without falsely reporting success.\n10. The black-screen interface activates after the configured inactivity period without ending SOS.\n11. PIN recovery follows the existing normal-PIN and duress-PIN behavior.\n12. Ending SOS stops supported recording and location updates.\n13. Tests verify important success, failure, permission-denial, and offline scenarios.\n14. The app builds successfully and can be installed on a real Android device.\n\nFor every item, report **PASS, FAIL, or NOT TESTED**. Do not mark a feature as working merely because the code compiles or the UI looks correct.\n\nAt the end, provide:\n- Files created or modified.\n- Exact commands to build and test the app.\n- Actual test results.\n- Android permissions or device setup required for testing.\n- Any Supabase schema, storage bucket, or function changes still needed.\n- Known limitations and incomplete functionality.\n\n**Work on Stage 2 now. Do not start Stage 3 until Stage 2 is verified.**"
    }
  }
