    end_line: 146
    content: "---\nname: \"app-icon-generation\"\ndescription: >-\n  Instructions for creating and configuring the App Icon for Android apps. Use\n  when you need to create a custom adaptive icon, generate a foreground image,\n  or configure the manifest for the app icon.\n---\n\n# App Icon\n\n## When To Generate\n\n-   **App Icon** \342\200\224 Mandatory for every new app. Replaces the default green\n    Android launcher icon.\n\n## App Icon Implementation\n\nCreate a custom **adaptive icon** following Material You guidelines. This\nreplaces the default launcher icon and must be set up for every new project.\n\n### Tool Usage & Workflow\n\nIf a `generate_image` tool is available, use it to create a custom foreground\nasset representing the app\'s core function instead of writing raw SVG paths.\n\n-   Generate only one foreground icon image related to the app\'s description.\n-   Do NOT use a transparent background color for the generated image.\n-   **Choose an explicit background hex color before calling the tool and\n    include it verbatim in the prompt** (e.g. \"... centered on a solid `#RRGGBB`\n    background\", substituting the color you chose for this app). The tool\n    returns only a file path; the generated pixels are never visible to you, so\n    this hex is your only reliable record of the asset\'s background color. Reuse\n    the exact same hex in `ic_launcher_background.xml` (see Requirement 2).\n\n**Fallback Rule:** If the `generate_image` tool is unavailable or fails (e.g.,\ndue to quota limits), you must manually create a custom Vector Drawable for the\nforeground layer. Choose a bold, filled icon from the core Material Icons\nlibrary that represents the app\'s purpose. Then complete Requirement 4\n(non-adaptive icons) using Option B in its example.\n\n### Requirements\n\n1.  **Foreground layer (`res/drawable/ic_launcher_foreground.xml`):**\n\n    -   **Custom Asset:** Do not use basic, thin-lined Material Symbols.\n        Generate a unique, bold, filled vector silhouette or use the generated\n        image asset that represents the app\'s core function for strong visual\n        weight.\n    -   **Implementation:** If using a `generate_image` tool, it saves the asset\n        in `app/src/main/res/drawable/` as either a `.jpg` or `.png` file\n        (depending on the image model\'s MIME type). **Overwrite the entire\n        `ic_launcher_foreground.xml` file** using a `<layer-list>` to center the\n        asset strictly inside a **66dp** safe zone (within the 108dp canvas):\n\n    ```xml\n    <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"utf-8\"?>\n    <layer-list xmlns:android=\"http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android\">\n        <item\n            android:width=\"66dp\"\n            android:height=\"66dp\"\n            android:drawable=\"@drawable/YOUR_GENERATED_IMAGE_NAME\"\n            android:gravity=\"center\" />\n    </layer-list>\n    ```\n\n2.  **Background layer (`res/drawable/ic_launcher_background.xml`):**\n\n    -   **If the foreground is a generated image:** fill the background with a\n        solid color using the **exact hex you pinned in the `generate_image`\n        prompt**. Do not use a gradient, and do not substitute theme colors from\n        `ui/theme/Color.kt`. If the hex was not pinned, read the saved image\n        file back and sample a corner pixel to recover it; never guess.\n    -   **If the foreground is a fallback vector silhouette:** the background is\n        fully visible, so pick a bold color that contrasts with the silhouette\n        and apply a subtle linear gradient (shifting gently diagonally from\n        top-left to bottom-right) between two shades of it. Explicitly avoid the\n        default Android \"Net Green.\"\n\n3.  **Adaptive wrapper (`res/mipmap-anydpi-v26/ic_launcher.xml` and\n    `ic_launcher_round.xml`):**\n\n    ```xml\n    <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"utf-8\"?>\n    <adaptive-icon xmlns:android=\"http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android\">\n        <background android:drawable=\"@drawable/ic_launcher_background\" />\n        <foreground android:drawable=\"@drawable/ic_launcher_foreground\" />\n    </adaptive-icon>\n    ```\n\n4.  **Non-adaptive raster fallback\n    (`res/mipmap-{mdpi,hdpi,xhdpi,xxhdpi,xxxhdpi}/ic_launcher.png` and\n    `ic_launcher_round.png`):**\n\n    -   If using a `generate_image` tool, **also save a valid PNG version of the\n        generated asset** to `ic_launcher.png` and `ic_launcher_round.png`\n        across all five density folders (`app/src/main/res/mipmap-mdpi/`,\n        `app/src/main/res/mipmap-hdpi/`, `app/src/main/res/mipmap-xhdpi/`,\n        `app/src/main/res/mipmap-xxhdpi/`, and\n        `app/src/main/res/mipmap-xxxhdpi/`). Note: if `generate_image` outputs a\n        `.jpg` file, convert it to real PNG format using ImageMagick `convert`\n        rather than raw-copying `.jpg` bytes to a `.png` filename, as AAPT2\n        enforces PNG header signature verification on `.png` resources.\n    -   If you do not use the `generate_image` tool (or it fails and you fall\n        back to a custom Vector Drawable), still replace the non-adaptive icons:\n        draw the same `pathData` with ImageMagick `-draw` (not via SVG, use\n        `#RRGGBB` colors) into a PNG and use it as `SRC` in the example below.\n    -   **`ic_launcher_round.png` must actually be round.** Launchers that use\n        `android:roundIcon` render this bitmap as-is without masking, so do not\n        copy the square asset to the `_round` filename. Mask the square asset to\n        an inscribed circle with transparent corners and save it as RGBA PNG.\n    -   **Delete** the default template `.webp` launcher files in all five\n        density folders (`app/src/main/res/mipmap-*/ic_launcher.webp` and\n        `app/src/main/res/mipmap-*/ic_launcher_round.webp`) to avoid AAPT2\n        duplicate resource conflicts and ensure Google Play\'s App Bundle\n        (`.aab`) ingestion extracts the custom PNG icon rather than the default\n        template icon.\n\n    Example handling all three steps across all densities with ImageMagick:\n\n    ```sh\n    # Option A: from generate_image\n    SRC=\"app/src/main/res/drawable/YOUR_GENERATED_IMAGE_FILE\"\n\n    # Option B (no generate_image / fallback vector): run these instead of Option A\n    # (affine 8,0,0,8,48,48 for 24x24 viewport; 4,0,0,4,-72,-72 for 108x108)\n    # SRC=\"/tmp/icon.png\"\n    # convert -size 288x288 xc:\'#BG\' -fill \'#FG\' \\\n    #   -draw \"affine 8,0,0,8,48,48 path \'<pathData>\'\" \"PNG32:$SRC\"\n    BASE=\"app/src/main/res\"\n    for spec in mdpi:48 hdpi:72 xhdpi:96 xxhdpi:144 xxxhdpi:192; do\n      dir=\"$BASE/mipmap-${spec%:*}\"\n      size=\"${spec#*:}\"\n      convert \"$SRC\" -resize \"${size}x${size}!\" \"PNG32:$dir/ic_launcher.png\"\n      [ -f \"$dir/ic_launcher.png\" ] && rm -f \"$dir/ic_launcher.webp\"\n      radius=$((size / 2))\n      convert \"$SRC\" -resize \"${size}x${size}!\" \\\n        \\( -size \"${size}x${size}\" xc:none -fill white \\\n           -draw \"circle $radius,$radius $radius,0\" \\) \\\n        -alpha set -compose DstIn -composite \"PNG32:$dir/ic_launcher_round.png\"\n      [ -f \"$dir/ic_launcher_round.png\" ] && rm -f \"$dir/ic_launcher_round.webp\"\n    done\n    ```\n\n5.  **Manifest:** Ensure `android:icon` and `android:roundIcon` in\n    `AndroidManifest.xml` point to `@mipmap/ic_launcher` and\n    `@mipmap/ic_launcher_round`.\n"
