  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Revisiting Keypad Code**\n\nI\'m back in `KeypadScreen.kt`. I\'m focusing on the `LaunchedEffect` block. Specifically, I\'m examining how the PIN verification logic is implemented after the user enters four digits. I\'m looking at how the `pinStorage.verify()` result is handled via a `when` block.\n\n\n**Analyzing Unlock Sequence**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the behavior when `onUnlockSuccess()` gets called. I see a race condition: `onUnlockSuccess()` is called immediately after `sosController.startSos(\"duress_pin\")`. This can result in potential UI inconsistencies. Specifically, `onUnlockSuccess()` navigates the user, but maybe not in a threadsafe way. I\'m focusing on the navigation behavior.\n\n\n**Investigating UI Navigation**\n\nI\'m now diving into the navigation flow from the `KeypadScreen` to the `TrackerMainScreen`. Specifically, when the navigation occurs using `navController.navigate(Screen.Tracker.route)`, I\'m checking how `KeypadScreen` is removed from the backstack using `inclusive = true`. I also realize there is a potential issue where the `TrackerMainScreen` isn\'t observing state changes reactively from the `sosController` if `sosController.state` isn\'t collected as a `StateFlow`. I\'m now exploring how to enable the SOS feature without alerting onlookers, as per requirements 8 and 6.\n\n\n**Examining SOS Trigger**\n\nI\'m now tracing the duress PIN execution path to understand why the SOS isn\'t visibly confirmed. I\'ve pinpointed the PIN verification function, the normal versus duress PIN logic, and the SOS controller entry point. I\'m focusing on the absence of any SOS status indicator within `SettingsTabView` or elsewhere in the application, even after a duress PIN entry. The intent of the duress feature is met, but the diagnostic flow is not.\n\n\n"
