    session_summary: "<summary>\n### 1. Task Overview\n- **Core Request**: Implement, test, and harden the native Android app **Cloak \342\200\224 The Invisible Guardian** (public disguise: **Petal**), structured in 3 stages:\n  1. Stage 1: Native foundation, secure PBKDF2 dual PINs (Normal 1234, Duress 9999), Support Circle (up to 3 contacts in Room DB), linear accelerometer shake detector, idempotent `SosController`, and native SMS with Google Maps links (`https://maps.google.com/?q=LAT,LNG`).\n  2. Stage 2: Complete \"Petal\" Period Tracker disguise (Calendar, Log, 15 Tips, Settings, simulated \"Call Gynecologist\" incoming call), ~5s audio chunks with `SosForegroundService` and Supabase Storage upload, and Fake-Sleep 15s inactivity blackout screen with 3s hold recovery.\n  3. Stage 3: Digital Shield (secret 3s logo press with 20px movement cancellation and haptic pulse, background auto-dismiss), Photo EXIF Sanitizer, Digital Lockdown system settings shortcuts, and atomic Burner Notes via Supabase RPC `read_and_burn`.\n- **Latest Specific Issue Being Addressed**:\n  - The user requested an analysis of the PIN verification logic and SOS controller entry point to ensure the duress PIN silently triggers the SOS workflow without exposing an obvious SOS screen.\n  - An architectural improvement was initiated: introduce `AuthViewModel` to handle PIN entry and verification, decoupled from the Composable lifecycle, and harden `SosForegroundService` against Android 14+ `SecurityException`s.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Progress\n- **Completed & Verified**:\n  - **Platform & Identity**: `metadata.json` configured as \"Petal\", `strings.xml` app_name is \"Petal\", and `settings.gradle.kts` rootProject is \"Cloak\".\n  - **Launcher Icons**: Custom petal floral emblem generated via `generate_image` (`petal_app_icon_1792849396758.jpg`), centered in 66dp layer-list (`ic_launcher_foreground.xml`), background `#F8BBD0` (`ic_launcher_background.xml`), and rasterized PNGs generated across mdpi\342\200\223xxxhdpi via ImageMagick.\n  - **Crypto & Security**: `CryptoManager.kt` with `PBKDF2WithHmacSHA256` (10,000 iterations, 16-byte random salt, constant-time `MessageDigest.isEqual`), `PinStorage.kt` for Normal vs. Duress PIN separation.\n  - **Local Database (Room v2)**: `CloakDatabase.kt` with `EmergencyContact` (max 3 enforced in `ContactRepository.kt`) and `PeriodEntry` (`PeriodDao.kt` & `PeriodRepository.kt`).\n  - **Hardware & Sensors**: `ShakeDetector.kt` using gravity-filtered linear acceleration (3 spikes $\\ge 18\\,\\text{m/s}^2$, $\\ge 250\\,\\text{ms}$ spacing within $1500\\,\\text{ms}$).\n  - **SOS & Telephony**: `SosController.kt` (centralized, idempotent), `SmsDispatcher.kt` (native `SmsManager` with Google Maps link), and `LocationProvider.kt` (`FusedLocationProviderClient`).\n  - **Disguise UI**: `TrackerMainScreen.kt` with bottom navigation (Calendar marking period in red and predictions in light pink, Log with symptom chips, 15 evidence-based wellness tips, Settings with Support Circle & Cycle slider).\n  - **Simulated Incoming Call**: `FakeCallScreen.kt` (\"Call Gynecologist\" card: tap for \"Mom\", long-press for \"Police\", realistic incoming call UI, ringtone, vibration, and live timer).\n  - **Fake-Sleep Mode**: `FakeSleepOverlay.kt` (15s inactivity blackout screen during active SOS, 3s hold-to-unlock, dual-PIN routing).\n  - **Digital Shield Suite**: `SecretGestureDetector.kt` (3s hold on logo, cancels if drift $> 20\\text{px}$, haptic pulse, disabled during SOS), `PhotoSanitizer.kt` (zero-permission Photo Picker, EXIF stripping, post-cleaning verification), `DigitalLockdown.kt` (system panel shortcuts for Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, Location, Airplane Mode), and `BurnerNoteRepository.kt` (6-digit code generator, 24h expiry, Supabase RPC `read_and_burn` atomic single-use deletion, rate limiting).\n  - **`AuthViewModel.kt`**: Created in `app/src/main/java/com/example/ui/auth/AuthViewModel.kt` to encapsulate 4-digit accumulation, error handling, normal PIN (`stopSos()`), and duress PIN (`startSos(\"duress_pin\")`).\n  - **Service Hardening**: `SosForegroundService.kt` updated to check `RECORD_AUDIO` and location permissions before passing foreground service types to `startForeground()` to avoid Android 14+ crashes.\n  - **Build & Tests**:\n    - Build succeeded (`compile_applet` passed).\n    - Debug APK assembled (`app-debug.apk` in `/app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk`).\n    - 23 unit & Robolectric tests passed across `Stage1SafetyEngineTest`, `Stage2DisguiseAndGuardianTest`, `Stage3DigitalShieldAndHardeningTest`, and `DuressPinAndSosActivationTest`.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Findings & Decisions\n- **Reactivity Issue**: In `TrackerMainScreen.kt`, `sosController.isSosActive` was originally read as an uncollected getter. Fixed by collecting `val sosState by sosController.state.collectAsState()` and `val isSosActive = sosState is SosState.Active`, ensuring `FakeSleepOverlay` and Settings status update reactively.\n- **Stealth Preservation**: In compliance with user constraint (\"Do not expose an obvious SOS banner, alert, or emergency screen on the disguised tracker\"), the main Calendar, Log, and Tips screens contain ZERO emergency indicators. A low-profile \"Guardian Protection\" card was placed exclusively inside Settings > Security so testers can verify background SOS status without blowing cover.\n- **Android 14+ FGS Constraint**: Starting `SosForegroundService` with `FOREGROUND_SERVICE_TYPE_MICROPHONE` or `LOCATION` throws `SecurityException` on Android 14+ if permissions are not granted prior to `startForeground()`. Dynamically checking permissions and falling back to base notification prevents service crashes.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Active Context\n- **Workspace State**:\n  - `KeypadScreen.kt` currently uses its internal `LaunchedEffect` for PIN entry, but `AuthViewModel.kt` has been created.\n  - Need to wire `KeypadScreen.kt` to `AuthViewModel` and verify compilation and test suite.\n- **Database / Backend**:\n  - `CloakDatabase` version 2 (`emergency_contacts` and `period_entries`).\n  - Supabase client configured with public anon key and project URL.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Next Steps\n1. **Wire `KeypadScreen.kt` to `AuthViewModel`**:\n   - Update `KeypadScreen.kt` to use `AuthViewModel` for key clicks, state collection, and unlock event emissions.\n2. **Verify Compilation & Test Suite**:\n   - Run `compile_applet` to confirm zero compilation errors.\n   - Run `gradle :app:testDebugUnitTest` to ensure all 23+ tests pass.\n   - Re-assemble debug APK (`gradle :app:assembleDebug`).\n3. **Report to User**:\n   - Deliver clear diagnosis of the PIN verification path in `AuthViewModel` and `SosController`.\n   - Provide test results confirming Normal PIN (no SOS), Duress PIN (silent SOS), identical tracker destination, and real-device limitation disclosures.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Commitments & Constraints\n- Must NOT expose any obvious SOS banners, alerts, or emergency screens on the public \"Petal\" tracker.\n- Both Normal PIN (1234) and Duress PIN (9999) MUST unlock into the identical Petal tracker interface.\n- Normal PIN must stop active SOS; Duress PIN must initiate or maintain active SOS.\n- Never use Supabase service-role keys or paid APIs in client code.\n- Report honest PASS/FAIL/NOT TESTED status for real hardware features (carrier SIM SMS, physical shake, GPS lock).\n</summary>"
    included_step_index_end: 358
    user_requests: "# CLOAK \342\200\224 BUILD THE COMPLETE END-TO-END NATIVE ANDROID MVP\n\nYou have already completed Stage 0 (architecture and feasibility planning) for my project, **Cloak \342\200\224 The Invisible Guardian**.\n\nNow proceed from the existing Stage 0 plan. Do not restart the project or repeat the architecture-only response.\n\nMy goal is a complete, integrated, runnable Android MVP with the core features from my original Cloak architecture, implemented in only THREE implementation stages.\n\n## 1. MANDATORY TECHNOLOGY AND PROJECT RULES\n\n- Build a genuine native Android application using Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, Android SDK, and Gradle.\n- Do not use Flutter, Dart, React Native, or a web app.\n- Use the separate Windows project location `D:\\CloakNative`.\n- Never modify or overwrite `D:\\Cloak\\cloak_app`, `D:\\CloakFresh\\cloak_app`, or `D:\\CloakFresh\\cloak_app_stage1_backup_20261024`.\n- Preserve working files. Inspect existing files before changing them.\n- Reuse the completed Stage 0 plan and the architecture decisions already made in this conversation.\n- Keep the project modular, buildable, testable, and suitable for opening in Android Studio.\n- Use Supabase free-tier services for the backend. Do not introduce mandatory paid services, Firebase Storage, paid SMS APIs, or paid maps APIs.\n- Never put Supabase service-role credentials or other private secrets in the Android application.\n- Do not claim any feature works unless its implementation and relevant tests support that claim.\n\n## 2. THE THREE IMPLEMENTATION STAGES\n\n### STAGE 1 \342\200\224 NATIVE FOUNDATION + CORE SAFETY ENGINE\n\nBuild a functioning native project, not just UI mockups.\n\nImplement:\n\n**Project and interface**\n- Android project structure, Gradle configuration, Compose theme, navigation, state management, and required manifest configuration.\n- A simple, polished tracker shell that both PIN paths can open.\n- A clear first-run setup flow.\n\n**Normal and duress PINs**\n- A custom four-digit PIN keypad.\n- Setup for separate normal and duress PINs.\n- Both PINs must open the same tracker interface.\n- Normal PIN must not trigger SOS.\n- Duress PIN must trigger the central SOS controller without displaying a visible warning or different screen.\n- Never use hardcoded demo PINs as production credentials.\n- Store PIN verifiers using a cryptographically secure password-based derivation function, a random salt, and constant-time verification. Never store plaintext PINs or use a fast unsalted hash.\n\n**Emergency contacts**\n- Support up to three emergency contacts.\n- Provide contact management and clear permission/error states.\n\n**SOS controller**\n- Implement one central, idempotent SOS state controller shared by the duress PIN and shake trigger.\n- Record the trigger source and maintain consistent SOS state.\n- Implement foreground shake detection using gravity-free accelerometer readings: three qualifying spikes, at least 250 ms apart, within 1.5 seconds. Start around 18 m/s\302\262 and make the threshold configurable.\n- Do not claim shake detection works when the app is killed or in the background unless verified and supported.\n\n**Location and emergency SMS**\n- Obtain last-known location, then request a fresh location when permitted.\n- Send an emergency SMS using native Android SMS functionality when permission and device support allow it.\n- Include a Google Maps URL in the message: `https://maps.google.com/?q=LATITUDE,LONGITUDE`.\n- Support up to three emergency contacts.\n- Update location approximately every two minutes during an active session when the Android lifecycle and permissions permit.\n- Handle denied permissions, unavailable location, missing SIM, and SMS errors without crashing.\n- The normal PIN stops SOS; the duress PIN returns to the tracker without stopping SOS.\n\n**Stage 1 completion requirements**\n- The application compiles.\n- The normal and duress PIN flows work.\n- Both flows display the same tracker shell.\n- Duress activation and shake activation call the same SOS controller.\n- Unit tests cover PIN verification, duress separation, repeated triggers, and SOS state transitions.\n- Provide exact commands and actual build/test results.\n\nDo not finish this stage with only generated code or screenshots. Make the project runnable before proceeding.\n\n### STAGE 2 \342\200\224 COMPLETE DISGUISE + GUARDIAN EXPERIENCE\n\nContinue from the existing Stage 1 project. Do not recreate it.\n\n**Period and health tracker**\n- Bottom navigation: Calendar, Log, Tips, Settings.\n- Calendar marks period days in red, predicted next period days in light pink, and symptoms with a dot.\n- Allow marking period start/end dates.\n- Cycle prediction uses the user\'s average cycle length, default 28 days.\n- Add symptom chips, mood logging, and 15 static health tips. Five tips are acceptable if necessary for a working MVP.\n- Include emergency contact management and discreet branding such as the public-facing name \342\200\234Petal\342\200\235.\n- Keep Cloak as the internal project name.\n\n**Fake incoming call**\n- Add a \342\200\234Call Gynecologist\342\200\235 card.\n- A normal tap can show a simulated incoming call from \342\200\234Mom\342\200\235; a long press can select \342\200\234Police\342\200\235.\n- Use an available ringtone where permitted and provide a fallback.\n- Do not imply that a real phone call is taking place.\n\n**Audio recording and Supabase**\n- During SOS, record approximately five-second audio chunks and upload them to Supabase Storage where Android permits recording.\n- Store SOS session metadata and location records in Supabase.\n- Add upload retry and offline error handling.\n- Respect Android microphone permissions, foreground-service requirements, notifications, and privacy indicators.\n- Android 14+ background microphone restrictions must be respected. Do not promise recording will continue invisibly in every background or screen-off state.\n- Keep SOS state independent of screen appearance.\n\n**Fake-Sleep mode**\n- After approximately 15 seconds of inactivity during active SOS, show a completely black screen.\n- Exit requires a three-second hold followed by PIN entry.\n- Normal PIN stops SOS; duress PIN leaves SOS active.\n- Make the screen lifecycle-safe and explain the conditions under which recording can continue.\n\n**Supabase security**\n- Provide schema/migrations for SOS sessions and location records.\n- Configure restrictive Row Level Security and suitable Storage policies.\n- Use a secure, documented authentication/session approach.\n- Never allow unrestricted public reads of location history or audio.\n- Never expose service-role credentials in client code.\n\n**Stage 2 completion requirements**\n- All Stage 1 functionality still works.\n- Tracker data and PIN behaviour are preserved.\n- Duress PIN and shake-to-SOS initiate the same integrated flow.\n- SMS, Maps links, location, and supported audio upload are connected to the same SOS session.\n- Test the supported flow on a real Android phone where available.\n- Report actual test results and all unverified behaviours.\n\n### STAGE 3 \342\200\224 DIGITAL SHIELD + HARDENING + FINAL MVP\n\nContinue from the existing project and preserve all working features.\n\n**Secret Shield entry**\n- Press and hold the top-left tracker logo for three full seconds.\n- Cancel if the finger moves more than 20 pixels or releases early.\n- Provide a short haptic confirmation.\n- Do not display hints, tooltips, or a normal Settings entry.\n- Disable entry while SOS is active.\n- Close the Shield and return to the root screen when the app enters the background.\n\n**Photo Sanitizer**\n- Let the user select a photo.\n- Remove EXIF metadata, especially GPS and device details.\n- Re-encode or otherwise create a genuinely clean copy.\n- Save the output appropriately and verify its metadata before reporting success.\n- Never claim metadata has been removed unless verification supports that claim.\n\n**Digital Lockdown**\n- Open Android system panels/settings for Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, and Location.\n- Android generally does not allow ordinary apps to silently toggle these settings. Implement compliant settings shortcuts rather than bypassing system restrictions.\n\n**Burner Notes**\n- Let the user write a note and receive a random six-digit retrieval code.\n- Store the note in Supabase.\n- Implement one-time read-and-delete atomically on the server.\n- Expire notes after 24 hours and reject expired or previously used codes.\n- Add rate limiting and restrictive RLS.\n- A second retrieval must report that the note has already been destroyed.\n- Do not expose private keys or unrestricted read access to notes.\n\n**Optional WhatsApp relay**\n- Provide a `wa.me` link for emergency communication.\n- Explain that opening WhatsApp and sending the message requires user interaction.\n\n**Final integration**\n- Review the whole codebase for missing wiring, placeholder implementations, duplicate controllers, unsafe permissions, and broken navigation.\n- Run all available automated tests and fix failures.\n- Verify the Gradle build.\n- Test normal PIN, duress PIN, SMS, location link, shake trigger, audio upload where supported, Fake-Sleep, simulated call, photo sanitization, settings shortcuts, and one-time Burner Notes.\n- Provide exact PowerShell commands for building, testing, and installing the APK.\n- Generate a release APK only after the relevant build succeeds.\n- Provide a 90-second demo script and a list of known limitations.\n\n## 3. INTEGRATION AND IMPLEMENTATION RULES\n\n1. Each stage must build on the same project. Never create a new unrelated project for a later stage.\n2. Complete the essential implementation and integration within each stage before moving on.\n3. Do not replace real functionality with buttons that merely display \342\200\234Success\342\200\235.\n4. Do not leave core features as TODOs, mock services, hardcoded responses, or UI-only simulations unless the feature is explicitly described as a simulation, such as the fake call.\n5. If a backend credential or permission is missing, build the proper integration, document the setup, and report the feature as awaiting configuration or verification.\n6. If a feature cannot be completed in the current environment, implement the best compliant version, identify the exact limitation, and give the precise remaining steps.\n7. Do not remove essential features silently to make the build pass.\n8. Prefer small, targeted file edits over regenerating the entire project.\n9. Do not assume a successful build or test. Report only checks actually performed.\n10. Keep the project and generated files easy to back up and continue if the AI usage limit is reached.\n\n## 4. REQUIRED STAGE REPORT\n\nAt the end of each stage, provide:\n\n- Stage status: complete and verified, partially complete, or blocked.\n- Files created or modified.\n- Exact build and test commands.\n- Actual test results and build output.\n- Features verified versus features still requiring real-device or Supabase configuration.\n- Known bugs and Android limitations.\n- Backup instructions and the next action.\n\n## 5. START NOW\n\nStage 0 planning is already complete.\n\nUse the existing Stage 0 decisions and begin implementing **Stage 1** now. Do not repeat the architecture plan and do not ask me to approve the same plan again.\n\nFirst inspect the current project workspace and available file-editing/build capabilities. Create or use only `D:\\CloakNative`. If the environment cannot write directly to that Windows directory, generate the project files in the supported workspace and provide a complete, clearly organized file set plus exact PowerShell instructions to transfer them safely.\n\nBuild, test, and report Stage 1 honestly before proceeding to Stage 2. Preserve the three-stage plan throughout development."
    user_requests: "# CLOAK \342\200\224 STAGE 2: PERIOD TRACKER DISGUISE + LIVE SOS FEATURES\n\nContinue working on my existing native Android application, **Cloak \342\200\224 The Invisible Guardian**, using Kotlin and Jetpack Compose.\n\n**Stage 0 is complete.** Preserve all verified work and existing architecture. Do not restart the project, replace working files unnecessarily, or repeat Stage 0.\n\nImplement **Stage 2 completely**, integrating with the Stage 1 code already present. First inspect the current project and identify the actual classes, permissions, services, and features that already exist. Reuse working implementations rather than creating duplicate systems.\n\n## 1. Primary requirement: Period-tracker disguise\n\nThe app must appear to be a normal period-tracking application from the Android home screen.\n\n- Use **Petal** as the public-facing app name.\n- Configure the Android launcher label and app icon to look like a period tracker, not a safety or SOS application.\n- Use a tasteful, simple menstrual-health icon and matching app theme.\n- When the app opens normally, immediately show the period-tracker interface.\n- Include bottom navigation: Calendar, Log, Tips, and Settings.\n- Calendar: mark period days and predict the next period using an average cycle length, defaulting to 28 days.\n- Log: record symptoms and mood.\n- Tips: show at least five basic period-health tips.\n- Settings: manage emergency contacts, cycle settings, and privacy options.\n- Keep emergency functionality accessible through the configured PIN and SOS workflows, not through an obvious SOS button on the public home screen.\n\nDo not leave the launcher name, launcher icon, splash screen, notifications, or app title visibly branded as \342\200\234Cloak\342\200\235 or \342\200\234SOS\342\200\235 in normal use. Do not impersonate another installed application.\n\n## 2. Emergency contacts: maximum three\n\nImplement contact management for up to three emergency contacts.\n\n- Allow adding, editing, deleting, and selecting contacts.\n- Store only the information needed for emergency delivery.\n- Validate phone numbers and handle missing or invalid contacts.\n- Request Android permissions at the appropriate time and explain why they are needed.\n- Ensure that the SOS controller uses the saved contacts consistently.\n- Handle unavailable SIM cards, missing network connectivity, permission denial, and SMS failures gracefully.\n\n## 3. Live location sharing\n\nImplement the real SOS location workflow for all configured emergency contacts, up to three.\n\nWhen SOS starts:\n\n1. Obtain the best available location using Android\'s supported location APIs.\n2. If a last-known location is available, send it promptly while attempting to obtain a fresh location.\n3. Send an SMS to each configured contact containing a Google Maps link in this format:\n\n   `https://maps.google.com/?q=LATITUDE,LONGITUDE`\n\n4. Include a timestamp and make clear whether the location is recent or last known.\n5. While the SOS session remains active, refresh the location approximately every two minutes when Android permits.\n6. Share updates with all configured contacts, with sensible rate limiting and retry handling.\n7. Stop location updates when the SOS controller confirms that the session has ended.\n\nUse native Android location APIs and the existing SOS controller. Do not use a paid Maps API or paid SMS provider.\n\nDo not claim that SMS or live tracking is guaranteed: delivery depends on permissions, SIM, carrier, network, and Android background-execution limits. Handle these limitations visibly in diagnostics.\n\n## 4. SOS audio recording in approximately five-second chunks\n\nIntegrate audio recording with the active SOS session.\n\n- Start recording only after the user has explicitly granted the required microphone permission and the SOS recording workflow has been activated.\n- Use a supported Android foreground-service approach where required, with the appropriate service type and notification.\n- Capture audio in approximately five-second chunks.\n- Upload each completed chunk securely to the existing Supabase backend.\n- Associate each chunk with the SOS session, a sequence number, and a timestamp.\n- Upload chunks as they are completed rather than waiting for the entire recording to finish.\n- Handle temporary network loss with a bounded retry queue and avoid duplicate uploads.\n- Protect audio using private storage and appropriate access controls.\n- Make the audio available to the authorized emergency workflow for the configured contacts. Do not expose public, unauthenticated audio URLs or send large audio files directly through SMS.\n- Provide an appropriate emergency notification or secure access mechanism for authorized contacts if the existing backend supports it.\n- Stop recording and release the microphone when SOS ends, subject to the defined safety workflow.\n\n**Important:** Android may restrict microphone access when an app is in the background. Implement only supported behavior and report failures honestly. Do not bypass Android permission prompts, microphone privacy indicators, or operating-system restrictions.\n\n## 5. Black-screen / Fake-Sleep interface during SOS\n\nImplement the configured Fake-Sleep interface for an active SOS session.\n\n- After approximately 15 seconds of inactivity during SOS, transition to a completely black app interface.\n- The black interface must not accidentally end the SOS session, stop supported recording, or stop location sharing.\n- Keep the active SOS controller independent of the visible screen.\n- Provide a controlled recovery path requiring a three-second press and PIN verification.\n- A normal PIN should stop SOS according to the existing architecture.\n- A duress PIN should return to the normal period-tracker interface while leaving the SOS session active.\n- Do not expose emergency details on the lock screen or in ordinary notifications.\n- Do not claim that a black app screen turns off the display, guarantees recording, or hides Android\'s system microphone indicators.\n\n## 6. Preserve Stage 1 security\n\nDo not weaken or replace the existing PIN security, duress PIN, contact storage, or idempotent SOS controller.\n\n- The normal PIN and duress PIN must continue to open the same period-tracker screen.\n- Entering the duress PIN must silently initiate the configured SOS workflow, without showing an obvious SOS screen.\n- Reuse the existing secure PIN hashing implementation.\n- Prevent repeated SOS initialization from duplicate events.\n- Ensure the black-screen interface and normal tracker UI do not accidentally reset an active SOS session.\n\n## 7. Supabase and privacy\n\nUse the existing Supabase configuration and schema wherever possible.\n\n- Store SOS sessions, location updates, and audio chunk metadata securely.\n- Use private storage buckets and restrictive Row Level Security policies.\n- Authorize contact access through the intended secure workflow.\n- Never put the Supabase service-role key in the Android app.\n- Never make sensitive audio or location records publicly readable.\n- Do not silently upload audio outside the explicitly activated SOS workflow.\n- Do not add Firebase Storage, paid services, or unnecessary dependencies.\n\n## 8. Stage 2 implementation requirements\n\nBefore coding, inspect the current project and produce a short inventory of:\n- Existing working Stage 1 components.\n- Existing Android permissions and manifest entries.\n- Existing Supabase configuration and backend functions.\n- Features that are missing or only placeholders.\n\nThen implement the missing Stage 2 functionality in the existing project.\n\nUpdate the launcher label and icon resources, Compose screens, Android manifest, location and recording services, SOS integration, Supabase integration, and tests as required.\n\nDo not create a fake implementation that merely changes the UI. The location-sharing, audio-chunk upload, contact handling, and SOS lifecycle must be wired to actual code.\n\n## 9. Verification checklist\n\nTest and report each item individually:\n\n1. The installed app displays **Petal** with a period-tracker icon on the home screen.\n2. Opening the app displays the tracker, not an SOS dashboard.\n3. Calendar, Log, Tips, and Settings work.\n4. Up to three emergency contacts can be configured.\n5. Starting SOS sends a location SMS to every configured contact when permissions and delivery conditions allow.\n6. Fresh location updates continue at the configured interval while supported.\n7. Audio is captured in approximately five-second chunks during an active, permitted SOS session.\n8. Each completed chunk is uploaded to private Supabase storage.\n9. Upload failures are handled without falsely reporting success.\n10. The black-screen interface activates after the configured inactivity period without ending SOS.\n11. PIN recovery follows the existing normal-PIN and duress-PIN behavior.\n12. Ending SOS stops supported recording and location updates.\n13. Tests verify important success, failure, permission-denial, and offline scenarios.\n14. The app builds successfully and can be installed on a real Android device.\n\nFor every item, report **PASS, FAIL, or NOT TESTED**. Do not mark a feature as working merely because the code compiles or the UI looks correct.\n\nAt the end, provide:\n- Files created or modified.\n- Exact commands to build and test the app.\n- Actual test results.\n- Android permissions or device setup required for testing.\n- Any Supabase schema, storage bucket, or function changes still needed.\n- Known limitations and incomplete functionality.\n\n**Work on Stage 2 now. Do not start Stage 3 until Stage 2 is verified.**"
    user_requests: "# CLOAK \342\200\224 STAGE 3: DIGITAL SHIELD, SECURITY HARDENING & FINAL APK\n\nContinue my existing native Android project, **Cloak \342\200\224 The Invisible Guardian**, built with Kotlin and Jetpack Compose.\n\nStage 0 is complete. Stage 1 and Stage 2 should already exist. **Do not restart the project, replace its architecture, or rewrite working components unnecessarily.** Inspect the actual source code and test results first.\n\nYour goal is to complete the remaining features, resolve integration issues, verify the entire app end to end, and prepare a working installable Android APK.\n\n## 1. First: audit Stages 1 and 2\n\nBefore making changes, inspect the current code and report the actual status of:\n\n- Petal launcher name and period-tracker icon.\n- Calendar, Log, Tips, and Settings.\n- Normal PIN and duress PIN.\n- Emergency contacts (up to three).\n- SOS controller and shake-to-SOS.\n- SMS location sharing to all configured contacts.\n- Location refresh while SOS remains active.\n- Approximately five-second audio chunks and private Supabase uploads.\n- Fake-Sleep black screen and PIN recovery.\n\nClassify each feature as **implemented and verified**, **implemented but unverified**, **incomplete**, or **missing**.\n\nFix any incomplete or broken Stage 1/2 integration before claiming the final app is ready. Do not replace real functionality with mock data or placeholder screens.\n\n## 2. Digital Shield: hidden activation\n\nImplement the Digital Shield entry gesture on the period tracker.\n\n- A three-second press on the top-left logo activates the Shield.\n- Cancel activation if the finger moves more than 20 pixels.\n- Provide a short haptic confirmation when activation succeeds.\n- Disable activation while an SOS session is active.\n- Cancel the gesture if the app backgrounds or the interaction is interrupted.\n- Do not display an obvious Shield or SOS button on the public tracker screen.\n\nTest both successful activation and cancellation cases.\n\n## 3. Photo EXIF sanitizer\n\nImplement a functional photo-cleaning feature.\n\n- Allow the user to select a photo using Android\'s supported photo picker.\n- Re-encode the selected photo and save a separate cleaned copy.\n- Remove location GPS coordinates and other unwanted EXIF metadata from the cleaned copy.\n- Verify the saved copy rather than assuming that re-encoding removed every metadata field.\n- Preserve the original photo unless the user explicitly chooses to delete it.\n- Handle unsupported formats, corrupt images, insufficient storage, and permission errors.\n- Clearly report whether cleaning and verification succeeded.\n\nDo not upload photos to an external service for processing.\n\n## 4. Digital Lockdown shortcuts\n\nCreate a Digital Lockdown screen with shortcuts that open Android\'s supported system settings or panels for:\n\n- Wi-Fi.\n- Bluetooth.\n- Location.\n\nUse official Android intents or settings panels where supported. Do not attempt to silently switch these settings on or off, bypass device restrictions, or claim that opening a panel changed a setting.\n\nProvide a clear fallback when a particular panel is unavailable.\n\n## 5. Burner Notes\n\nImplement one-time Burner Notes using the existing Supabase backend.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Generate a random six-digit retrieval code.\n- Allow the sender to create a note with a 24-hour expiry.\n- Store the note securely in Supabase.\n- Permit one authorized successful retrieval only.\n- Atomically mark the note as consumed or delete it so two simultaneous requests cannot both retrieve it.\n- Enforce expiry on the server, not only in the Android UI.\n- Apply rate limiting to code attempts and retrieval requests.\n- Avoid exposing note content in logs, analytics, notifications, or public URLs.\n- Use restrictive Row Level Security policies and server-side authorization.\n- Never embed the Supabase service-role key in the Android app.\n- Never make the notes table publicly readable.\n- Show useful errors for expired, already-read, invalid, or unavailable notes.\n\nImplement the atomic retrieval and authorization through an appropriate secure server-side function or database operation. A client-side check followed by a separate delete is not sufficient.\n\nDocument any Supabase migrations, policies, storage configuration, or functions that must be deployed before this feature can work.\n\n## 6. Security and privacy review\n\nAudit the entire application.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- PINs are never stored as plaintext.\n- The existing secure PIN hashing and duress-PIN logic remain intact.\n- SOS initialization is idempotent.\n- Normal PIN recovery stops SOS as intended.\n- Duress PIN recovery returns to Petal while SOS remains active.\n- Sensitive location and audio records are not publicly accessible.\n- Supabase service-role secrets are never included in the APK.\n- No secrets are committed to Git or written to ordinary logs.\n- Contact data and temporary audio files are handled carefully.\n- Temporary files are cleaned up when safe to do so.\n- Permissions are requested only when needed and denial is handled gracefully.\n- The app does not falsely report SMS delivery, audio upload, or location sharing as successful.\n- No functionality bypasses Android\'s privacy indicators, permission prompts, or background execution limits.\n\nDo not introduce unnecessary dependencies or paid services.\n\n## 7. End-to-end SOS verification\n\nTest the complete workflow on a real Android device wherever possible:\n\n1. Launch Petal from the home screen.\n2. Verify the period-tracker icon and launcher label.\n3. Enter the normal PIN and confirm that the tracker opens.\n4. Enter the duress PIN and confirm that the tracker opens while SOS starts.\n5. Verify location SMS attempts for each configured emergency contact, up to three.\n6. Verify that location updates continue at the configured interval while Android permits.\n7. Verify that audio is captured in approximately five-second chunks during a permitted active SOS session.\n8. Verify that completed chunks reach private Supabase storage and that failed uploads are reported accurately.\n9. Verify that Fake-Sleep activates after the configured inactivity period without ending SOS.\n10. Verify the three-second recovery gesture and both PIN behaviors.\n11. Verify that ending SOS stops supported recording and location updates.\n12. Verify that shake-to-SOS follows its configured foreground behavior.\n13. Verify Digital Shield activation and gesture cancellation.\n14. Verify EXIF cleaning by inspecting the saved file\'s metadata.\n15. Verify Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, and Location settings shortcuts.\n16. Verify Burner Notes expiry, single-use retrieval, invalid codes, rate limiting, and concurrent retrieval attempts.\n17. Verify the app behaves safely after permission denial, network loss, process interruption, and backend unavailability.\n\nFor every test, report **PASS**, **FAIL**, or **NOT TESTED**. Distinguish unit tests, emulator tests, and real-device tests.\n\nDo not claim real SMS delivery, background recording, secure one-time retrieval, or successful uploads unless the relevant behavior has actually been tested.\n\n## 8. Automated tests and build quality\n\n- Run all existing tests and preserve useful coverage.\n- Add tests for the Digital Shield gesture, photo-cleaning logic, Burner Notes validation, SOS state transitions, and failure handling.\n- Add or update backend tests for expiry and atomic one-time retrieval where supported.\n- Resolve compilation errors, broken imports, manifest issues, and failing tests.\n- Check that no debug-only mock behavior is being presented as production functionality.\n- Confirm that configuration and secrets are handled safely.\n\nUse the build system already configured in the project. Do not migrate build systems without a demonstrated need.\n\n## 9. Final APK and handover\n\nWhen the code is ready:\n\n1. Build a debug APK for installation and testing.\n2. If signing configuration is already available and safe, document the release-build process. Do not invent signing credentials or include secrets in source control.\n3. Provide the actual APK output location and exact build command.\n4. Provide exact commands for running tests.\n5. List every file created or modified.\n6. List required Android permissions.\n7. List Supabase SQL migrations, RLS policies, storage buckets, and functions that still need deployment.\n8. Provide a short real-device installation and testing guide.\n9. Report remaining limitations honestly.\n\nIf the environment cannot create or export an APK, explain precisely what is missing and provide the source/build commands needed to produce it locally.\n\n## Completion rules\n\n- Preserve the Petal period-tracker disguise.\n- Preserve the existing Stage 1/2 architecture and working safety features.\n- Do not claim that a feature is complete just because a screen exists.\n- Do not fabricate test results or device verification.\n- Do not start a new project.\n- Do not begin additional features after the requested MVP is complete.\n\n**Complete Stage 3, verify the whole application, and provide a final readiness report.**"
    user_requests: "## BUG FIX: DURESS PIN OPENS PETAL BUT SOS STATUS IS UNCLEAR\n\nIn my current Cloak Android app, entering the duress PIN opens the period-tracker screen, just like the normal PIN.\n\nThe visible tracker screen is intentional. However, according to my architecture, entering the duress PIN must also silently activate the SOS workflow.\n\nPlease inspect the existing code and fix this behavior without changing the intended disguise.\n\n### Required behavior\n\n1. Entering the normal PIN opens the Petal period tracker without activating SOS.\n2. Entering the duress PIN opens the exact same Petal period tracker while activating the SOS controller.\n3. SOS activation must not depend on which screen is visible.\n4. Ensure the SOS controller starts only once per activation.\n5. Verify that the active SOS state persists when navigating between screens or entering Fake-Sleep.\n6. Verify that the SOS workflow attempts the configured emergency actions, including location acquisition, SMS to configured contacts, and audio recording when the necessary permissions and Android execution conditions permit.\n7. Do not claim SMS delivery, location sharing, or audio upload succeeded unless it is verified.\n8. Do not expose an obvious SOS banner, alert, or emergency screen on the disguised tracker.\n9. Add automated tests proving that the normal PIN does not activate SOS and the duress PIN does activate SOS while both return to the same tracker screen.\n\n### Diagnose before modifying\n\nIdentify the PIN verification function, the normal-versus-duress PIN decision, and the SOS controller entry point. Trace the duress PIN execution path and explain why SOS is not visibly or functionally confirmed.\n\nMake the smallest safe changes necessary. Do not rebuild the app or replace existing working architecture.\n\nAfter fixing, run the relevant tests and report:\n- Whether normal PIN activates SOS: PASS/FAIL.\n- Whether duress PIN activates SOS: PASS/FAIL.\n- Whether both PINs open the same tracker: PASS/FAIL.\n- Whether the SOS controller is genuinely invoked: PASS/FAIL.\n- Which real device features cannot be verified in the AI Studio preview.\n\nDo not mark the fix complete merely because the tracker screen appears."
    user_requests: "Analyze the existing PIN verification logic in the ViewModel and the SOS controller\'s entry point to identify why the duress PIN isn\'t triggering the SOS workflow silently. Ensure the verification step correctly invokes the SOS controller when a duress PIN is matched."
    render_latest_checkpoint: true
    total_subagent_count: 0
  }
}
