  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Keypad Screen**\n\nI\'m now diving into `KeypadScreen.kt` and `AuthViewModel.kt`. Currently, I\'m examining `KeypadScreen.kt`. It takes `pinStorage`, `sosController`, and `onUnlockSuccess` as input parameters. This seems to be the entry point for PIN code interaction.\n\n\n**Refactoring Keypad Screen**\n\nI\'ve been exploring the possibility of incorporating a default `AuthViewModel` within `KeypadScreen`. The proposed code snippet looks promising. The use of `remember` and `LaunchedEffect` seems clean and efficient. Furthermore, the handling of `KeypadButton` clicks and the `verifyPin` logic within the `AuthViewModel` seem elegant and robust. The `pinStorage.verify(pin)` logic looks to be the key.\n\n\n**Interpreting Pin Verification**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how different PIN verification outcomes are handled within the `AuthViewModel`. Specifically, I\'m analyzing the branching logic within `verifyPin`, which deals with `NormalPinSuccess`, `DuressPinSuccess`, and `InvalidPin`. I\'m paying close attention to the `sosController` interactions. If `NormalPinSuccess`, the SOS is stopped if active and emits `_unlockEvent`. If `DuressPinSuccess`, the SOS is started with a \"duress\\_pin\" trigger, and also emits `_unlockEvent`. Invalid pins trigger an error message and clear the entered PIN.\n\n\n"
