  planner_response {
    response: "### Diagnosis: Duress PIN & Silent SOS Execution Path\n\n#### 1. Architecture & Execution Trace\n- **PIN Verification Engine (`PinStorage.kt`)**: Uses PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA256 (10,000 iterations, 16-byte random salt, constant-time `MessageDigest.isEqual`) to independently match inputs against the Normal PIN hash and Duress PIN hash. It returns a sealed `PinVerificationResult` (`NormalPinSuccess`, `DuressPinSuccess`, or `InvalidPin`).\n- **Decoupled State Management (`AuthViewModel.kt`)**: Digit accumulation (4 digits) and validation dispatch are isolated in `AuthViewModel`.\n- **Decision & Dispatch**:\n  - **Normal PIN**: Calls `if (sosController.isSosActive) sosController.stopSos()`, halts any active foreground service, and emits `unlockEvent`.\n  - **Duress PIN**: Silently executes `sosController.startSos(triggerSource = \"duress_pin\")` before emitting `unlockEvent`.\n  - Both outcomes navigate to the identical disguise destination: `Screen.Tracker.route` (`TrackerMainScreen`).\n- **Why SOS is Not Visibly or Functionally Confirmed**:\n  - **Intended Stealth**: The public \"Petal\" interface (Calendar, Log, Tips) deliberately displays **zero** SOS banners, emergency text, or siren icons. \n  - **Verification Channel**: In `TrackerMainScreen.kt`, active SOS is observed reactively via `sosController.state.collectAsState()`. During an active SOS session, the background controller starts `SosForegroundService` with periodic location updates, native SMS dispatch to all configured emergency contacts (up to 3), ~5s audio recording chunks uploaded to Supabase Storage, and armed Fake-Sleep blackout mode (15s inactivity). For testing and auditing, the background status is visible under **Settings > Guardian Protection Status** without exposing cover.\n\n---\n\n### Verification & Test Results\n\n| Requirement / Test Check | Result | Verification Details |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Normal PIN does not activate SOS** | **PASS** | Entering normal PIN (1234) unlocks to Petal while SOS remains strictly `SosState.Idle` (`isSosActive = false`). If an existing SOS was active, it terminates immediately. |\n| **Duress PIN activates SOS** | **PASS** | Entering duress PIN (9999) transitions `SosController` to `SosState.Active(triggerSource = \"duress_pin\")` with a unique session ID. |\n| **Both PINs open the same tracker** | **PASS** | Both verification branches resolve to `Screen.Tracker.route`, presenting the identical \"Petal\" period-tracker UI with zero visible alarms. |\n| **SOS controller is genuinely invoked** | **PASS** | `SosController.startSos(\"duress_pin\")` is idempotently invoked, starting foreground services, location acquisition, and emergency SMS dispatch to saved contacts. |\n\n---\n\n### Real-Device Features That Cannot Be Verified in Emulator / Preview\n\n1. **Carrier SIM SMS Dispatch**: Native cellular SMS dispatch via Android `SmsManager` requires an active physical SIM card with cellular network carrier provisioning. In cloud/preview environments, SMS dispatch invokes Android framework APIs, but actual over-the-air cellular transmission cannot execute without a real baseband radio and SIM.\n2. **Physical Shake Sensor Telemetry**: Accelerometer threshold triggering (3 spikes $\\ge 18\\,\\text{m/s}^2$ spaced $\\ge 250\\,\\text{ms}$ within $1500\\,\\text{ms}$) requires physical device movement; in the preview emulator, sensors supply static gravity values.\n3. **Live GPS Hardware Fix**: While `FusedLocationProviderClient` executes successfully, real-time satellite GPS triangulation requires an open-sky physical antenna.\n4. **Hardware Vibrator & Haptic Actuator**: Physical haptic feedback (`Vibrator` / `VibratorManager`) for the 3-second Digital Shield hold and simulated call ringing requires a physical vibration motor."
    thinking: "**Analyzing Test Results**\n\nI\'ve just successfully compiled and built the debug version! Now, I\'m diving into the test results. Twenty-six tests ran, and thankfully, none failed. Specifically, I\'m examining `DuressPinAndSosActivationTest` to verify that `normalPin_unlocksToTrackerWithoutActivatingSos` performed as expected.\n\n\n**Examining Feature Interactions**\n\nI\'m now shifting focus to the interactions between different features, specifically how the duress PIN impacts the SOS functionality and tracker screens, based on several test descriptions. I\'m scrutinizing `duressPin_activatesSosAndOpensSameTrackerScreen`, `duressPin_repeatedEntryMaintainsExistingSessionIdempotently`, and `normalPin_stopsActiveSosSessionAndReturnsToTracker`. The tests like `activeSos_persistsAcrossFakeSleepAndScreenNavigation`, help confirm expected behavior across different states, now I\'m connecting all the tests together.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "### Diagnosis: Duress PIN & Silent SOS Execution Path\n\n#### 1. Architecture & Execution Trace\n- **PIN Verification Engine (`PinStorage.kt`)**: Uses PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA256 (10,000 iterations, 16-byte random salt, constant-time `MessageDigest.isEqual`) to independently match inputs against the Normal PIN hash and Duress PIN hash. It returns a sealed `PinVerificationResult` (`NormalPinSuccess`, `DuressPinSuccess`, or `InvalidPin`).\n- **Decoupled State Management (`AuthViewModel.kt`)**: Digit accumulation (4 digits) and validation dispatch are isolated in `AuthViewModel`.\n- **Decision & Dispatch**:\n  - **Normal PIN**: Calls `if (sosController.isSosActive) sosController.stopSos()`, halts any active foreground service, and emits `unlockEvent`.\n  - **Duress PIN**: Silently executes `sosController.startSos(triggerSource = \"duress_pin\")` before emitting `unlockEvent`.\n  - Both outcomes navigate to the identical disguise destination: `Screen.Tracker.route` (`TrackerMainScreen`).\n- **Why SOS is Not Visibly or Functionally Confirmed**:\n  - **Intended Stealth**: The public \"Petal\" interface (Calendar, Log, Tips) deliberately displays **zero** SOS banners, emergency text, or siren icons. \n  - **Verification Channel**: In `TrackerMainScreen.kt`, active SOS is observed reactively via `sosController.state.collectAsState()`. During an active SOS session, the background controller starts `SosForegroundService` with periodic location updates, native SMS dispatch to all configured emergency contacts (up to 3), ~5s audio recording chunks uploaded to Supabase Storage, and armed Fake-Sleep blackout mode (15s inactivity). For testing and auditing, the background status is visible under **Settings > Guardian Protection Status** without exposing cover.\n\n---\n\n### Verification & Test Results\n\n| Requirement / Test Check | Result | Verification Details |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Normal PIN does not activate SOS** | **PASS** | Entering normal PIN (1234) unlocks to Petal while SOS remains strictly `SosState.Idle` (`isSosActive = false`). If an existing SOS was active, it terminates immediately. |\n| **Duress PIN activates SOS** | **PASS** | Entering duress PIN (9999) transitions `SosController` to `SosState.Active(triggerSource = \"duress_pin\")` with a unique session ID. |\n| **Both PINs open the same tracker** | **PASS** | Both verification branches resolve to `Screen.Tracker.route`, presenting the identical \"Petal\" period-tracker UI with zero visible alarms. |\n| **SOS controller is genuinely invoked** | **PASS** | `SosController.startSos(\"duress_pin\")` is idempotently invoked, starting foreground services, location acquisition, and emergency SMS dispatch to saved contacts. |\n\n---\n\n### Real-Device Features That Cannot Be Verified in Emulator / Preview\n\n1. **Carrier SIM SMS Dispatch**: Native cellular SMS dispatch via Android `SmsManager` requires an active physical SIM card with cellular network carrier provisioning. In cloud/preview environments, SMS dispatch invokes Android framework APIs, but actual over-the-air cellular transmission cannot execute without a real baseband radio and SIM.\n2. **Physical Shake Sensor Telemetry**: Accelerometer threshold triggering (3 spikes $\\ge 18\\,\\text{m/s}^2$ spaced $\\ge 250\\,\\text{ms}$ within $1500\\,\\text{ms}$) requires physical device movement; in the preview emulator, sensors supply static gravity values.\n3. **Live GPS Hardware Fix**: While `FusedLocationProviderClient` executes successfully, real-time satellite GPS triangulation requires an open-sky physical antenna.\n4. **Hardware Vibrator & Haptic Actuator**: Physical haptic feedback (`Vibrator` / `VibratorManager`) for the 3-second Digital Shield hold and simulated call ringing requires a physical vibration motor."
