  user_input {
    user_response: "# CLOAK \342\200\224 DEMO MODE: VISIBLE SOS ACTIVATION, THEN FAKE-SLEEP\n\nModify my existing native Android Kotlin + Jetpack Compose project. Do not restart the project or replace its architecture.\n\n## Objective\n\nFor my hackathon demonstration, when I enter the duress PIN, I want judges to see a brief SOS status screen showing location-sharing and audio-recording status. After the short demonstration period, the app must transition automatically to the black Fake-Sleep screen while the active SOS session continues.\n\nThe public-facing app must still look like a period tracker named **Petal** on the Android home screen and during normal use.\n\n## 1. Preserve normal and duress PIN behavior\n\n- Normal PIN: open the Petal period tracker and do not activate SOS.\n- Duress PIN: activate the central SOS controller and open the same tracker initially.\n- In an explicitly enabled **Demo Mode**, after the duress PIN activates SOS, display the temporary SOS demonstration screen described below.\n- In normal production mode, retain the configured discreet behavior and do not display an obvious SOS screen.\n- Do not hardcode PINs or weaken the existing PIN hashing and verification.\n\n## 2. Temporary visible SOS screen for the demo\n\nCreate a polished, simple Compose screen that appears immediately after successful duress-PIN verification when Demo Mode is enabled.\n\nDisplay a heading such as **Emergency assistance active**, followed by these three status rows:\n\n1. **Location sharing**\n   - Show up to three configured emergency contacts.\n   - Show each contact\'s delivery status separately.\n   - Use honest statuses such as `Getting location`, `Sending SMS`, `SMS submitted`, `Failed`, or `Waiting for permission`.\n   - If a valid location is available, show its timestamp and a Maps link.\n   - Never display `Delivered` unless actual delivery confirmation is available.\n\n2. **Voice recording**\n   - Show `Starting recording`, `Recording`, `Permission required`, or `Recording unavailable`, according to the actual service state.\n   - Show the most recent approximately five-second chunk\'s upload status, such as `Uploading`, `Uploaded`, `Retrying`, or `Failed`.\n   - Update this status from real recording and upload events, not a simulated animation.\n   - Request microphone permission only through Android\'s supported permission flow.\n\n3. **SOS session**\n   - Show whether the SOS controller is active.\n   - Display the elapsed session time if available.\n   - Keep the SOS controller independent of this screen\'s lifecycle.\n\nUse a clean, professional interface that judges can understand quickly. Do not show private PINs, API keys, or unnecessary sensitive information.\n\n## 3. Automatic transition to Fake-Sleep\n\n- After approximately **8 seconds**, transition automatically to the existing black Fake-Sleep interface.\n- Make the delay configurable for demonstrations.\n- The transition must not stop SOS, recording, location updates, SMS processing, or chunk uploads.\n- Do not use a UI timer as the SOS controller or recording lifecycle.\n- If the SOS controller is not active, do not falsely display a successful SOS session.\n- If permissions are missing or services fail, display the truthful status during the visible demonstration period.\n\n## 4. Demo Mode must be explicit\n\n- Add a clear developer/demo configuration that can be enabled for hackathon demonstrations and disabled for normal use.\n- Do not make Demo Mode automatically active in a production release.\n- Do not expose a conspicuous SOS button on the normal period-tracker home screen.\n- Demo Mode must not bypass Android permissions, privacy indicators, or background-service restrictions.\n- Never use fake contacts, fabricated GPS coordinates, simulated successful SMS delivery, or fabricated recording/upload status in a real integration test.\n- If a demo-only simulation is needed, label it clearly as simulated and keep it separate from the real SOS workflow.\n\n## 5. Preserve Fake-Sleep recovery\n\n- Keep the screen completely black while Fake-Sleep is active.\n- SOS must continue independently of the visible UI wherever Android permits.\n- Preserve the existing three-second recovery gesture and PIN verification.\n- Normal PIN recovery stops SOS according to the current architecture.\n- Duress PIN recovery returns to Petal while leaving SOS active.\n\n## 6. Tests and acceptance criteria\n\nAdd or update tests to verify:\n\n1. Normal PIN opens Petal without starting SOS.\n2. Duress PIN activates the SOS controller.\n3. Demo Mode displays the temporary SOS screen.\n4. The screen reflects actual location, SMS, recording, and upload states.\n5. The screen transitions to Fake-Sleep after the configured delay.\n6. The transition does not stop the active SOS session.\n7. Production mode preserves discreet behavior.\n8. Permission denial and service failure show accurate status rather than false success.\n\nRun the relevant tests and report PASS, FAIL, or NOT TESTED for each item. Distinguish UI tests from real-device tests.\n\nDo not rewrite working Stage 1 or Stage 2 features. Make the smallest safe changes, and report the exact files modified and any remaining limitations."
    items {
      text: "# CLOAK \342\200\224 DEMO MODE: VISIBLE SOS ACTIVATION, THEN FAKE-SLEEP\n\nModify my existing native Android Kotlin + Jetpack Compose project. Do not restart the project or replace its architecture.\n\n## Objective\n\nFor my hackathon demonstration, when I enter the duress PIN, I want judges to see a brief SOS status screen showing location-sharing and audio-recording status. After the short demonstration period, the app must transition automatically to the black Fake-Sleep screen while the active SOS session continues.\n\nThe public-facing app must still look like a period tracker named **Petal** on the Android home screen and during normal use.\n\n## 1. Preserve normal and duress PIN behavior\n\n- Normal PIN: open the Petal period tracker and do not activate SOS.\n- Duress PIN: activate the central SOS controller and open the same tracker initially.\n- In an explicitly enabled **Demo Mode**, after the duress PIN activates SOS, display the temporary SOS demonstration screen described below.\n- In normal production mode, retain the configured discreet behavior and do not display an obvious SOS screen.\n- Do not hardcode PINs or weaken the existing PIN hashing and verification.\n\n## 2. Temporary visible SOS screen for the demo\n\nCreate a polished, simple Compose screen that appears immediately after successful duress-PIN verification when Demo Mode is enabled.\n\nDisplay a heading such as **Emergency assistance active**, followed by these three status rows:\n\n1. **Location sharing**\n   - Show up to three configured emergency contacts.\n   - Show each contact\'s delivery status separately.\n   - Use honest statuses such as `Getting location`, `Sending SMS`, `SMS submitted`, `Failed`, or `Waiting for permission`.\n   - If a valid location is available, show its timestamp and a Maps link.\n   - Never display `Delivered` unless actual delivery confirmation is available.\n\n2. **Voice recording**\n   - Show `Starting recording`, `Recording`, `Permission required`, or `Recording unavailable`, according to the actual service state.\n   - Show the most recent approximately five-second chunk\'s upload status, such as `Uploading`, `Uploaded`, `Retrying`, or `Failed`.\n   - Update this status from real recording and upload events, not a simulated animation.\n   - Request microphone permission only through Android\'s supported permission flow.\n\n3. **SOS session**\n   - Show whether the SOS controller is active.\n   - Display the elapsed session time if available.\n   - Keep the SOS controller independent of this screen\'s lifecycle.\n\nUse a clean, professional interface that judges can understand quickly. Do not show private PINs, API keys, or unnecessary sensitive information.\n\n## 3. Automatic transition to Fake-Sleep\n\n- After approximately **8 seconds**, transition automatically to the existing black Fake-Sleep interface.\n- Make the delay configurable for demonstrations.\n- The transition must not stop SOS, recording, location updates, SMS processing, or chunk uploads.\n- Do not use a UI timer as the SOS controller or recording lifecycle.\n- If the SOS controller is not active, do not falsely display a successful SOS session.\n- If permissions are missing or services fail, display the truthful status during the visible demonstration period.\n\n## 4. Demo Mode must be explicit\n\n- Add a clear developer/demo configuration that can be enabled for hackathon demonstrations and disabled for normal use.\n- Do not make Demo Mode automatically active in a production release.\n- Do not expose a conspicuous SOS button on the normal period-tracker home screen.\n- Demo Mode must not bypass Android permissions, privacy indicators, or background-service restrictions.\n- Never use fake contacts, fabricated GPS coordinates, simulated successful SMS delivery, or fabricated recording/upload status in a real integration test.\n- If a demo-only simulation is needed, label it clearly as simulated and keep it separate from the real SOS workflow.\n\n## 5. Preserve Fake-Sleep recovery\n\n- Keep the screen completely black while Fake-Sleep is active.\n- SOS must continue independently of the visible UI wherever Android permits.\n- Preserve the existing three-second recovery gesture and PIN verification.\n- Normal PIN recovery stops SOS according to the current architecture.\n- Duress PIN recovery returns to Petal while leaving SOS active.\n\n## 6. Tests and acceptance criteria\n\nAdd or update tests to verify:\n\n1. Normal PIN opens Petal without starting SOS.\n2. Duress PIN activates the SOS controller.\n3. Demo Mode displays the temporary SOS screen.\n4. The screen reflects actual location, SMS, recording, and upload states.\n5. The screen transitions to Fake-Sleep after the configured delay.\n6. The transition does not stop the active SOS session.\n7. Production mode preserves discreet behavior.\n8. Permission denial and service failure show accurate status rather than false success.\n\nRun the relevant tests and report PASS, FAIL, or NOT TESTED for each item. Distinguish UI tests from real-device tests.\n\nDo not rewrite working Stage 1 or Stage 2 features. Make the smallest safe changes, and report the exact files modified and any remaining limitations."
    }
  }
}
