    thinking: "**Analyzing Demo Mode Functionality**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specifics of SOS behavior within Demo Mode. I\'m focusing on the data exposure requirements and also re-reading user documentation to clarify any ambiguities. I want to make sure I am providing the user with appropriate SOS activation and a \"fake sleep\" feature.\n\n\n**Refining SOS Demo Sequence**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the specific actions that need to happen in demo mode. The duress PIN will trigger a visible SOS status screen, which presents location sharing and audio recording status. However, after the demonstration window ends, it needs to transition seamlessly to the \"Fake-Sleep\" screen. Crucially, the public-facing app appearance and underlying PIN behavior must remain unchanged. I\'m focusing on ensuring this transition is fluid.\n\n\n**Designing Demo Mode Display**\n\nI\'m now designing the visual presentation for the demo mode SOS screen, focusing on clarity and honesty. My priority is conveying critical information like location sharing and voice recording status to the user. I\'m focusing on providing direct, actionable feedback. Specifically, I plan on displaying the heading **Emergency assistance active**, and the following rows: Location sharing and Voice recording. The statuses will be \"honest\" and not misleading.\n\n\n**Implementing SOS Details**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the specific data to display during the SOS session in demo mode. I plan to clearly show whether the SOS controller is active and display the elapsed time. Also, I must ensure that the SOS controller remains independent of the on-screen display. I\'m prioritizing a clean, professional interface and will avoid showing private or sensitive information. I\'m also ensuring uploads of data, instead of simulated animations. I will also request the microphone permission only through Android\'s supported permission flow.\n\n\n\n**Clarifying Demo Mode**\n\nI am now focusing on the essential aspects of demo mode. It will require a specific developer configuration. Critically, I must ensure demo mode is explicitly enabled, not automatically active. I will also be sure to hide the SOS button from the main screen. The demo will not bypass system permissions. Furthermore, all test simulations will be clearly labeled. Fake-Sleep will also be handled with a black screen, preserving the existing PIN recovery mechanism.\n\n\n**Defining Test Criteria**\n\nI\'m now outlining a comprehensive testing plan. I\'m prioritizing verifying the behavior of the system, under specific conditions. I will be sure to address scenarios, such as normal and duress PIN entries, as well as demo mode activation and deactivation. Furthermore, the goal is to confirm seamless transitions between SOS and Fake-Sleep screens, which is key to a smooth user experience. Finally, I will ensure the system\'s accuracy and integrity through thorough testing.\n\n\n**Configuring Demo Mode**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how demo mode settings will be configured. I\'m placing the control in the \"Settings\" section, within \"Developer / Security options\" in `TrackerMainScreen.kt`. It will have a clear card for \"Hackathon Demo Mode\", with a toggle and a slider for transition delay, backed by a `DemoConfigManager` for persistence. There is also a concise explanatory note.\n\n\n**Detailing Contact Display**\n\nI\'m now detailing the display of emergency contacts within the SOS screen in demo mode. I\'ll show up to three configured contacts, with individual delivery statuses like \'Getting location,\' \'Sending SMS,\' or \'Failed.\' If location is available, I will provide a timestamp and Google Maps link. I\'ll be sure to never indicate \'Delivered\' unless confirmed, and use \'Permission required\' appropriately.\n\nI\'m also working on showing voice recording statuses accurately - from \'Starting recording\' to \'Failed.\' I will show the upload status of the most recent chunk and update it directly from real events, and have the elapsed SOS session time also visible. Finally, I will also keep the SOS controller independent.\n\n\n**Enhancing SOS State Display**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the internal data structures that govern the SOS status display, specifically within `SosState.Active`. I plan to introduce new data elements for clearer representation of contact SMS statuses, audio recording states, and chunk upload states. My goal is a more accurate depiction of the SOS session. I intend to include elapsed time and last Maps URL.\n\n\n**Refining Data Structures**\n\nI\'m now refining the `SosState.Active` data structure by adding observable fields. These new fields will track detailed statuses. I\'m focusing on the `contactStatuses`, `locationTimestampMs`, `audioRecordingStatus`, `chunkUploadStatus`, and permission flags. I\'m also reviewing `SosForegroundService` and `AudioChunkRecorder` to integrate their event reporting into this structure for accurate status updates.\n\n\n**Defining Data Flows**\n\nI\'m now detailing data flows between various components to ensure accurate SOS status updates in demo mode. I\'m focusing on handling audio recording and SMS events to update the `SosState.Active` data structure with accurate statuses. These will include microphone permissions, upload status of audio chunks, and SMS sending statuses. Each contact status will be updated as it progresses through each step of the SOS process. I am focusing on using specific data structures to accurately model statuses.\n\n\n**Refining Duress PIN Handling**\n\nI\'m currently revising the authentication flow to better distinguish between normal and duress PIN entries. The goal is to enhance the SOS response in demo mode. I am integrating an `AuthUnlockResult` sealed class, which represents the type of successful unlock, so that the main screen can behave more correctly. I am also working on adding a better implementation that ensures the demo mode functionality behaves appropriately upon a duress unlock in the UI. I am confident that these changes will offer a smoother user experience.\n\n\n**Clarifying Navigation Logic**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the navigation flow, particularly the transitions between demo mode\'s SOS screen and the Fake-Sleep or main Tracker screen. I am mapping out conditional navigations based on the unlock type (normal or duress) and current screen state. I am considering cases where the user is already on the Tracker screen or unlocks from the Fake-Sleep overlay, and the 8-second countdown in the demo is also a key factor.\n\n\n**Finalizing Screen Display**\n\nI\'m now detailing the display of the `DemoSosScreen`. The screen will have a header indicating the mode and timer. Location and recording statuses will be clearly displayed, showing real-time event updates. I will ensure accurate state changes during data processing, and during transition to \"Fake-Sleep.\" I also plan to ensure a high degree of fidelity in the transitions.\n\n\n**Finalizing Data Display**\n\nI\'m now detailing the user interface elements for the `DemoSosScreen` with a strong focus on accurate information delivery. I will provide contact statuses with GPS timestamp and clickable Google Maps links if the location is available. Voice recording status is also included, with status indicators and indicators for chunk upload, and the elapsed SOS session time. I will use a separate controller status and a trigger source indicator. Also, a \'Transition to Fake-Sleep Now\' button is included, and a transition to the Fake-Sleep screen after a user-configured delay, and the screen will transition to black.\n\n\n**Verifying State Transitions**\n\nI\'m now confirming the SOS controller and foreground service remain active, regardless of UI transitions from normal or duress PIN entries in demo mode. The `SosController` and `SosForegroundService` maintain their lifecycle independently, and the UI transitions do not affect either of them. I\'m also ensuring the demo mode configuration adheres to requirements, with explicit activation and no bypassing of permissions or restrictions. I\'m also confirming the status of fake data.\n\n\n"
